"""Saints and saints' days for Sky Paschalion (generated from the research JSON files; see SOURCES.md)."""

SAINTS = {
    's_abanoub': {'en': 'St Abanoub', 'text': 'Abanoub (Apanoub) of Nahisa was, according to Coptic tradition, a boy of about twelve who confessed Christ during the Diocletianic persecution and was put to death after many tortures. He is one of the most popular child martyrs in Egypt. His relics are kept at Samanoud in the Nile Delta.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abanoub', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/11_24']},
    's_abraam_of_fayoum': {'en': 'St Abraam of Fayoum', 'text': 'Abraam (1829-1914) was Coptic bishop of the Fayoum and Giza. He was famed for his poverty and for giving everything he received to the poor, and many miracles were attributed to his prayers. He was proclaimed a saint by the Coptic Orthodox Church in 1964.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraam,_Bishop_of_Faiyum', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/10_3']},
    's_abreha_and_atsbeha': {'en': 'Sts Abreha and Atsbeha', 'text': 'Abreha and Atsbeha are the names under which Ethiopian tradition remembers the fourth-century brother kings of Aksum, identified with Ezana and Saizana, in whose reign Frumentius brought Christianity to the kingdom. They are venerated as the first Christian kings of Ethiopia. A famous rock-hewn church in Tigray bears their names.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ezana_of_Axum', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Tekemt#Tekemt4']},
    's_addai_apostle': {'en': 'St Addai (Thaddeus) the Apostle', 'text': 'Addai, identified with Thaddeus, one of the Seventy (in some traditions one of the Twelve), is held by tradition to have been sent to Edessa after the Ascension, where he healed King Abgar and founded the Church. The Syriac churches, and the Church of the East in particular, honour him as their founding apostle, and the main East Syriac anaphora bears his name and that of his disciple Mari.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Addai_of_Edessa', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liturgy_of_Addai_and_Mari']},
    's_agatha': {'en': 'St Agatha', 'text': 'Agatha was a virgin martyr of Catania in Sicily, put to death probably in the persecution of Decius around 251. She is named in the Roman Canon of the Mass, and Catania keeps her feast with one of the largest processions in Italy. Tradition holds that her veil stopped an eruption of Mount Etna, so she is invoked against fire and volcanoes.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agatha_of_Sicily']},
    's_agnes': {'en': 'St Agnes', 'text': 'Agnes was a young Roman martyr, put to death probably in the persecution of Diocletian around 304, and buried on the Via Nomentana, where a basilica stands over her tomb. She is one of the virgin martyrs named in the Roman Canon of the Mass. On her day two lambs are blessed in Rome, and their wool is woven into the pallia that the Pope gives to metropolitan archbishops.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agnes_of_Rome']},
    's_albert_the_great': {'en': 'St Albert the Great', 'text': 'Albert (c. 1200–1280), a German Dominican friar and bishop of Regensburg, taught at Paris and Cologne, where St Thomas Aquinas was his student. He wrote on theology, philosophy, astronomy, botany, zoology and mineralogy and helped bring Aristotle into Christian thought. A Doctor of the Church called the Universal Doctor, he is patron of natural scientists.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albertus_Magnus']},
    's_alexander_nevsky': {'en': 'St Alexander Nevsky', 'text': 'Prince of Novgorod and Grand Prince of Vladimir (1221-1263) who defeated the Swedes on the Neva in 1240 and the Teutonic Knights on the ice of Lake Peipus in 1242, while negotiating with the Golden Horde to spare his people. He took monastic vows before his death; Peter the Great moved his relics to St Petersburg.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Nevsky']},
    's_alexis_toth': {'en': 'St Alexis Toth', 'text': 'Rusyn Greek Catholic priest (1854-1909) who, after a dispute with the Latin Catholic bishop of St Paul in 1889, led his Minneapolis parish into the Russian Orthodox mission and helped bring many Carpatho-Rusyn immigrants into Orthodoxy. Glorified in 1994, he is called Defender of Orthodoxy in America.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexis_Toth']},
    's_all_saints_of_russia': {'en': 'All Saints of Russia', 'text': 'Commemoration of all the saints who shone in the Russian land, known and unknown, first gathered in councils of 1547 and 1549 and restored as a feast in 1918. The Russian Church keeps it on the second Sunday after Pentecost, the Sunday after All Saints.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feast_of_All_Saints_of_Russia']},
    's_aloysius_gonzaga': {'en': 'St Aloysius Gonzaga', 'text': 'Aloysius Gonzaga (1568–1591) was the eldest son of an Italian noble family who renounced his inheritance to join the Jesuits in Rome. While a student he nursed victims of a plague epidemic and died of the disease aged twenty-three. He is patron of young people and students.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aloysius_Gonzaga']},
    's_alphonsa': {'en': 'St Alphonsa', 'text': 'Alphonsa Muttathupadathu (1910-1946) was a Syro-Malabar nun of the Franciscan Clarist Congregation at Bharananganam in Kerala, who bore long years of illness with patience. Many favours were attributed to her intercession after her death, and in 2008 she became the first woman of Indian origin to be canonised by the Catholic Church. Her tomb at Bharananganam is a major pilgrimage site.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alphonsa_Muttathupadathu']},
    's_alphonsus_liguori': {'en': 'St Alphonsus Liguori', 'text': "Alphonsus Maria de' Liguori (1696–1787), a Neapolitan lawyer turned priest, founded the Redemptorists to preach to the rural poor of southern Italy and later became bishop of Sant'Agata dei Goti. His Moral Theology, combining firmness with pastoral mildness, made him the patron of confessors and moral theologians. He also wrote popular devotional books and hymns, and he is a Doctor of the Church.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alphonsus_Liguori']},
    's_ambrose': {'en': 'St Ambrose', 'text': 'Ambrose (c. 339–397), the Roman governor at Milan, was acclaimed bishop of the city by the people in 374 while still unbaptised. He defended Nicene orthodoxy, resisted imperial pressure, introduced congregational hymn singing to the West and baptised St Augustine in 387. He is one of the four original Latin Doctors of the Church and gives his name to the Ambrosian Rite of Milan; 7 December is the day of his episcopal ordination.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambrose']},
    's_andrew': {'en': 'St Andrew', 'text': "Andrew, a fisherman of Bethsaida and brother of Simon Peter, was first a disciple of John the Baptist and is called in the East the Protokletos, the 'First-called' of the apostles. Tradition holds that he preached around the Black Sea and in Greece and was crucified at Patras, on an X-shaped cross in later tradition. The Ecumenical Patriarchate of Constantinople regards him as its founder, and he is patron of Scotland, Russia, Ukraine and Romania.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_the_Apostle', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/4_4']},
    's_andrew_dung_lac_and_companions': {'en': 'Sts Andrew Dung-Lac and Companions', 'text': 'Andrew Dung-Lac (1795–1839), a Vietnamese diocesan priest, is the representative of 117 martyrs, among them Vietnamese priests and lay people and Spanish and French missionaries, who were killed in Vietnam between 1745 and 1862. They were canonised together in 1988 as the Vietnamese Martyrs. Historians estimate that tens of thousands of Vietnamese Christians died for their faith in those persecutions.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnamese_Martyrs']},
    's_andrew_kim_and_companions': {'en': 'Sts Andrew Kim Tae-gon, Paul Chong Ha-sang and Companions', 'text': 'The Korean Church began in the late eighteenth century through lay people who studied Christian books from China. During the persecutions of 1839–1867 thousands died; the 103 canonised in 1984 include Andrew Kim Tae-gon, the first Korean priest, beheaded in 1846, and the lay apostle Paul Chong Ha-sang. They are honoured together as the Korean Martyrs.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_Martyrs']},
    's_anne_and_joachim': {'en': 'Sts Joachim and Anne', 'text': "Joachim and Anne are, by tradition, the parents of the Virgin Mary and grandparents of Jesus. Their names and story, including Mary's birth after long childlessness, come from the second-century Protoevangelium of James, not from the New Testament. Churches of East and West honour them as models of faithful married life, and Anne is especially popular as a patron of mothers and grandparents.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Anne', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joachim', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Joachim']},
    's_anthony_great': {'en': 'St Anthony the Great', 'text': 'Anthony (c. 251–356) was an Egyptian Christian who gave away his inheritance and withdrew into the desert, living for decades as a hermit near the Red Sea. His Life, written by St Athanasius, spread the monastic ideal across the Christian world, and he is called the Father of Monks. He is usually shown with a staff, a bell and sometimes a pig, and in many places animals are blessed on his day.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_the_Great', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/5_22']},
    's_anthony_of_kyiv': {'en': 'St Anthony of the Kyiv Caves', 'text': "Monk (983-1073) from near Chernihiv who after living on Mount Athos settled in a cave above the Dnieper around 1051, where the community of the Kyiv Caves Lavra gathered around him. He is honoured as the founder of monasticism in Rus'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_of_Kiev']},
    's_anthony_of_padua': {'en': 'St Anthony of Padua', 'text': "Anthony (1195–1231), born Fernando in Lisbon, became a Franciscan friar hoping for martyrdom in Morocco, but became instead a celebrated preacher in Italy and France. He died at Padua, where his basilica is a major shrine, and was canonised within a year. A Doctor of the Church, he is widely invoked to find lost things, and 'St Anthony's bread' for the poor is a custom in his honour.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_of_Padua']},
    's_archangels_gabriel_and_michael': {'en': 'Holy Archangels Gabriel and Michael', 'text': 'Gabriel, the messenger of the Annunciation, and Michael, the leader of the heavenly host, are the two archangels named in the canonical Scriptures of most churches. Their joint feast honours them together with all the angelic orders. Both are invoked as protectors, and many churches are dedicated to them.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabriel', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_(archangel)']},
    's_aregawi': {'en': 'St Aregawi (Za-Mika’el)', 'text': "Abuna Aregawi was the best known of the Nine Saints, monks from the Roman East who came to Aksum in the late fifth century and spread monasticism in Ethiopia. He founded the monastery of Debre Damo, a clifftop site that tradition holds he reached by grasping a serpent's tail. The 14th of every Ethiopian month is dedicated to him.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abuna_Aregawi', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Tekemt#Tekemt14']},
    's_armenian_genocide_martyrs': {'en': 'Holy Martyrs of the Armenian Genocide', 'text': 'The Armenian Apostolic Church canonised the victims of the Armenian Genocide of 1915-1923 on 23 April 2015, the eve of its centenary, as martyrs who died for faith and homeland. It is the only commemoration in the Armenian calendar of saints kept on a fixed civil date. On 24 April, the anniversary of the 1915 arrests in Constantinople, prayers are offered in all churches, and in Yerevan crowds walk to the Tsitsernakaberd memorial.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/100th_anniversary_of_the_Armenian_genocide', 'https://wdacna.com/en/feasts']},
    's_arsema': {'en': 'St Arsema (Rhipsime)', 'text': 'Arsema is the Ethiopian name of Rhipsime, a consecrated virgin who, according to tradition, fled Roman persecution and was martyred with her companions by King Tiridates of Armenia around 300. Her cult is very popular in Ethiopia, where many churches and pilgrimages are dedicated to her. The 29th of every Ethiopian month carries her commemoration.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hripsime', 'https://eotcmk.org/e/saint-arsema-2/', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Meskerem#Meskerem29']},
    's_arseni_of_konevets': {'en': 'St Arsenius of Konevets', 'text': 'Monk from Novgorod (d. 1447) who after a stay on Mount Athos founded the monastery of Konevets on an island in Lake Ladoga around 1393. He is honoured as one of the Enlighteners of Karelia.', 'sources': ['https://ort.fi/kirkkokalenteri/20250612/']},
    's_astius': {'en': 'St Astius of Dyrrachium', 'text': 'Bishop of Dyrrachium (Durrës) on the Adriatic coast who, tradition holds, was crucified under Trajan around 100. He is honoured as the patron of Durrës and a founding saint of the Church in Albania.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astius']},
    's_athanasius': {'en': 'St Athanasius', 'text': "Athanasius (c. 296–373) was patriarch of Alexandria and the leading defender of the Council of Nicaea's teaching that the Son is of one being with the Father, against Arianism. He was exiled five times for his stand. His writings include On the Incarnation and the Life of Anthony, and his Easter letter of 367 is the earliest list of exactly the 27 books of the New Testament. He is called the Apostolic and a pillar of Orthodoxy.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athanasius_of_Alexandria', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/9_7']},
    's_athanasius_athonite': {'en': 'St Athanasius the Athonite', 'text': 'Byzantine monk (c. 920-1003) who in 963 founded the Great Lavra, the first great monastery of Mount Athos, with the support of the emperor Nikephoros Phokas. His typikon organised communal life on the Holy Mountain, and he is honoured as the father of Athonite monasticism; tradition holds that he died when a church dome collapsed during construction.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athanasius_the_Athonite']},
    's_athanasius_paulose': {'en': 'St Athanasius Paulose', 'text': 'Paulose Mor Athanasius (1869-1953) was the metropolitan of Angamaly and from 1918 leader of the faction of the Malankara Church loyal to the Syriac Orthodox Patriarch of Antioch, popularly called Valiya Thirumeni. He is buried at Aluva and was declared a saint by the Syriac Orthodox Church in 2004.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athanasius_Paulose']},
    's_augustine': {'en': 'St Augustine of Hippo', 'text': "Augustine (354–430), born at Thagaste in Roman North Africa, was a teacher of rhetoric who, after years with the Manichaeans and Neoplatonists, was baptised by St Ambrose at Milan in 387. As bishop of Hippo he wrote the Confessions, The City of God and works on the Trinity, grace and the Church that shaped Western theology more than any other writer after the apostles. He is a Doctor of the Church in the West, and the Orthodox Church also commemorates him, often as 'Blessed Augustine'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augustine_of_Hippo']},
    's_barbara': {'en': 'St Barbara', 'text': 'Barbara was, according to tradition, a young woman of the 3rd century who was imprisoned in a tower by her pagan father and beheaded by him for her Christian faith. She is invoked against sudden death and is a patron of miners, gunners and firefighters. In Lebanon and Syria her feast is kept with boiled wheat and masks, somewhat like Halloween.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Barbara', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/4_8', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eid_il-Burbara']},
    's_barnabas': {'en': 'St Barnabas', 'text': "Barnabas, a Levite from Cyprus named Joseph, was given his name, meaning 'son of encouragement', by the apostles. As told in the Acts of the Apostles, he introduced the newly converted Paul to the Church of Jerusalem and travelled with him on the first missionary journey. Though not one of the Twelve, he is called an apostle; tradition holds that he was martyred at Salamis and he is the founder and patron of the Church of Cyprus.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barnabas', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/4_21']},
    's_barsoum': {'en': 'St Barsoum (Mor Barsawmo)', 'text': "Barsawmo (died 457) was a Syrian ascetic from Samosata who led a large community of monks and became one of the most influential holy men of his time. He took part in the Second Council of Ephesus in 449 and was a firm opponent of the Council of Chalcedon. The Syriac Orthodox Church honours him as 'head of the mourners' (chief of the ascetics); the Patriarchate was long based at his monastery near Melitene.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barsauma', 'http://syriacorthodoxresources.org/Feast/']},
    's_bartholomew': {'en': 'St Bartholomew the Apostle', 'text': 'Bartholomew, one of the Twelve and often identified with Nathanael of Cana, is traditionally said to have preached in India, Armenia and other lands. Tradition holds that he was flayed and martyred. He is commemorated as an apostle and martyr.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bartholomew_the_Apostle', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/1_1']},
    's_baselios_yeldho': {'en': 'St Baselios Yeldho', 'text': 'Baselios Yeldho was a Maphrian (Catholicos) of the Syriac Orthodox Church from near Mosul who travelled to Malabar at an advanced age in 1685 to support the Saint Thomas Christians. He died at Kothamangalam a few days after his arrival and is buried in the Marthoman church there, now a major pilgrimage centre. He is venerated by both the Malankara Jacobite and the Malankara Orthodox churches.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baselios_Yeldo', 'https://mosc.in/saints/st-baselios-yeldho-kothamangalam-bava/']},
    's_basil_great': {'en': 'St Basil the Great', 'text': "Archbishop of Caesarea in Cappadocia (c. 330-379), one of the three Cappadocian Fathers. He defended the divinity of the Holy Spirit against the Arians, wrote rules that still shape Eastern monasticism, and founded a large complex for the poor and sick. A Divine Liturgy bearing his name is served on several great days of the year, and in Greek homes the New Year 'Vasilopita' cake is cut in his honour.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basil_of_Caesarea', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/5_6']},
    's_basil_of_ostrog': {'en': 'St Basil of Ostrog', 'text': 'Serbian bishop of Zahumlje (1610-1671) who defended his flock under Ottoman rule and spent his last years in the cliff-top monastery of Ostrog in Montenegro. His relics there draw Orthodox, Catholic and Muslim pilgrims who seek healing.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basil_of_Ostrog']},
    's_bede': {'en': 'St Bede the Venerable', 'text': "Bede (c. 673–735) was a monk of the twin monastery of Wearmouth–Jarrow in Northumbria. His Ecclesiastical History of the English People is the main source for early English history, and his works on chronology spread the custom of dating years 'Anno Domini'. Declared a Doctor of the Church in 1899, he was the first English-born holder of that title.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bede']},
    's_beheading_of_john_the_baptist': {'en': 'Beheading of St John the Baptist', 'text': 'This day commemorates the death of John the Baptist, the forerunner of Christ, who was imprisoned and beheaded by Herod Antipas after condemning his marriage to Herodias, as told in the Gospels of Mark and Matthew. The date is traditionally linked to the dedication of a church at Sebaste in Samaria that was said to hold his relics. In the Roman Calendar it is called the Passion of St John the Baptist.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beheading_of_John_the_Baptist']},
    's_behnam_and_sarah': {'en': 'Sts Behnam, Sarah and the Forty Martyrs', 'text': 'Tradition holds that Behnam and his sister Sarah were children of an Assyrian king in the 4th century who were converted by the hermit Mar Mattai after Sarah was healed of leprosy, and were then put to death by their father together with forty companions. The Mar Behnam monastery near Qaraqosh in Iraq, built over their tomb, is a major pilgrimage site for Syriac Christians.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Behnam,_Sarah,_and_the_Forty_Martyrs', 'https://www.vaticannews.va/en/world/news/2025-12/new-from-the-orient-december-19-2025.html']},
    's_benedict': {'en': 'St Benedict', 'text': "Benedict of Nursia (c. 480–547) left his studies in Rome to live as a hermit at Subiaco and later founded the monastery of Monte Cassino. His Rule for monks, balancing prayer, work and study later summed up in the motto 'ora et labora', became the basis of Western monastic life. He is honoured as the father of Western monasticism and, in the Catholic Church, as the principal patron of Europe.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_of_Nursia']},
    's_bernard_of_clairvaux': {'en': 'St Bernard of Clairvaux', 'text': 'Bernard (1090–1153), a Burgundian noble, entered the reformed monastery of Cîteaux and became founding abbot of Clairvaux, the most influential Cistercian house. His sermons on the Song of Songs and his devotion to the Virgin Mary shaped medieval spirituality, and he was an adviser to popes and kings and preacher of the Second Crusade. He is a Doctor of the Church called the Mellifluous Doctor.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernard_of_Clairvaux']},
    's_blaise': {'en': 'St Blaise', 'text': 'Blaise was a bishop of Sebaste in Armenia, martyred in the early fourth century. Tradition holds that he saved a boy who was choking on a fishbone, so he became the patron of throat ailments. On his day Catholics receive the blessing of throats, given with two crossed candles held under the chin.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Blaise']},
    's_bonaventure': {'en': 'St Bonaventure', 'text': 'Bonaventure (1221–1274), born Giovanni di Fidanza near Viterbo, taught theology at Paris and was elected Minister General of the Franciscans, guiding the order through a difficult period. He wrote a Life of St Francis and The Journey of the Mind into God. He was made a cardinal and died during the Second Council of Lyon; he is a Doctor of the Church called the Seraphic Doctor.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonaventure']},
    's_boniface': {'en': 'St Boniface', 'text': 'Boniface (c. 675–754), born Wynfrith in Wessex, was an English Benedictine monk sent by the Pope as missionary to the Germanic peoples. He organised the Church in Germany as archbishop of Mainz and founded the abbey of Fulda, where he is buried. He was killed by raiders in Frisia, and he is called the Apostle of Germany.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Boniface']},
    's_boris_and_gleb': {'en': 'Sts Boris and Gleb', 'text': "Young princes of Kyiv, sons of St Vladimir, killed in 1015 in the struggle for the throne after they chose not to resist their brother by force. As the first saints canonised by the Church of Rus' they are honoured as 'passion-bearers', who accepted death in imitation of Christ's non-resistance.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boris_and_Gleb']},
    's_boris_of_bulgaria': {'en': 'St Tsar Boris-Michael of Bulgaria', 'text': 'Ruler of Bulgaria (r. 852-889) who was baptised in 864 with the name Michael, made Christianity the faith of the realm and welcomed the disciples of Cyril and Methodius, making Bulgaria a centre of Slavonic letters. He ended his days as a monk and is honoured as baptiser of Bulgaria and Equal-to-the-Apostles.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boris_I_of_Bulgaria']},
    's_brancoveanu_martyrs': {'en': 'Sts Constantin Brâncoveanu and his sons (the Brâncoveanu Martyrs)', 'text': 'Prince of Wallachia Constantin Brâncoveanu (1654-1714), a great patron of Orthodox culture, was beheaded in Constantinople with his four sons and his counsellor Ianache after refusing to renounce his faith. The Romanian Church glorified them as martyrs in 1992.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constantin_Brâncoveanu']},
    's_bridget_of_sweden': {'en': 'St Bridget of Sweden', 'text': "Bridget (Birgitta, 1303–1373) was a Swedish noblewoman, wife and mother of eight who, after her husband's death, devoted herself to prayer and recorded her visions in the Revelations. She founded the Order of the Most Holy Saviour (Bridgettines) and lived her last years in Rome, urging the popes to return from Avignon. She is a co-patron of Europe.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridget_of_Sweden']},
    's_callinicus_of_cernica': {'en': 'St Callinicus of Cernica', 'text': 'Romanian monk (1787-1868), abbot of the Cernica monastery near Bucharest and later Bishop of Râmnic, remembered for his life of prayer, fasting and care for the poor and for building churches and schools. He was among the first saints glorified by the Romanian Orthodox Church, in 1955, and his relics rest at Cernica.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Callinicus_of_Cernica']},
    's_catherine_of_alexandria': {'en': 'St Catherine of Alexandria', 'text': 'Catherine was, by tradition, a learned young woman of Alexandria who confounded pagan philosophers in debate and was martyred in the early fourth century after being condemned to be broken on a spiked wheel. Her relics are venerated at the Monastery of St Catherine at Mount Sinai. She is called Great-martyr in the East and was one of the most popular saints of medieval Europe, patron of philosophers, students and wheelwrights.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_of_Alexandria']},
    's_catherine_of_siena': {'en': 'St Catherine of Siena', 'text': 'Catherine Benincasa (1347–1380), a Dominican tertiary of Siena, was a mystic who also nursed the sick and worked for peace between Italian cities. Her letters helped persuade Pope Gregory XI to return the papacy from Avignon to Rome, and her Dialogue is a classic of spiritual writing. She is a Doctor of the Church, patron of Italy and co-patron of Europe.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_of_Siena']},
    's_cecilia': {'en': 'St Cecilia', 'text': 'Cecilia was a Roman virgin martyr, probably of the third century, whose church in Trastevere stands on the traditional site of her house. Her legendary Acts say that she sang to God in her heart at her wedding, which made her patron of musicians and church music. She is named in the Roman Canon of the Mass.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cecilia_of_Rome']},
    's_chair_of_peter': {'en': 'Chair of St Peter', 'text': "This feast honours St Peter's mission as teacher and shepherd of the Church, symbolised by the bishop's chair (cathedra), and through him the ministry of the Bishop of Rome. Its Roman origins go back to at least the fourth century. In St Peter's Basilica a wooden chair encased in Bernini's bronze 'Cathedra Petri' is the visible symbol of the feast.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chair_of_Saint_Peter']},
    's_charbel': {'en': 'St Charbel Makhlouf', 'text': 'Charbel Makhlouf (1828-1898) was a Lebanese Maronite monk and priest who spent the last 23 years of his life as a hermit at the hermitage of Sts Peter and Paul above the monastery of St Maron at Annaya. After his death many healings were reported at his tomb, which remains one of the most visited pilgrimage sites in Lebanon. He was canonised in 1977 and is the best-known Maronite saint.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charbel_Makhlouf']},
    's_charles_borromeo': {'en': 'St Charles Borromeo', 'text': 'Charles Borromeo (1538–1584), nephew of Pope Pius IV, was a cardinal and archbishop of Milan who helped conclude the Council of Trent and put its reforms into practice. He founded seminaries, visited every parish of his huge diocese and personally cared for the sick during the plague of 1576. He became a model of the reforming bishop.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Borromeo']},
    's_charles_lwanga_and_companions': {'en': 'Sts Charles Lwanga and Companions', 'text': 'Charles Lwanga and twenty-one companions, Catholic pages and officials at the court of King Mwanga II of Buganda, were killed between 1885 and 1887, most of them burned alive at Namugongo on 3 June 1886; Anglican converts died with them. They were canonised in 1964 as the Uganda Martyrs. Their shrine at Namugongo draws huge pilgrimages every 3 June.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uganda_Martyrs']},
    's_clare': {'en': 'St Clare', 'text': 'Clare of Assisi (1194–1253), a noblewoman inspired by St Francis, left her family in 1212 to follow Christ in poverty and founded the Order of Poor Ladies (Poor Clares) at San Damiano. Her Rule was the first written by a woman for women religious. Tradition holds that she repelled attacking soldiers by holding up the Eucharist; she is patron of television.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clare_of_Assisi']},
    's_clement_of_ohrid': {'en': 'St Clement of Ohrid', 'text': 'Disciple of Cyril and Methodius (c. 840-916) who after 886 taught thousands of pupils in the Ohrid region and became the first bishop of the Bulgarian Church to use the Slavonic language. He is credited with shaping the Cyrillic alphabet and is honoured by Bulgarians and Macedonians as an enlightener.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clement_of_Ohrid']},
    's_constantine_and_helen': {'en': 'Sts Constantine and Helen', 'text': 'Constantine the Great (d. 337), the first Christian Roman emperor, ended the persecution, called the First Council of Nicaea and founded Constantinople; his mother Helen (d. c. 330) made a pilgrimage to the Holy Land, where tradition holds that she found the True Cross. The Eastern churches honour both as Equals-to-the-Apostles.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constantine_the_Great', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helena,_mother_of_Constantine_I']},
    's_conversion_of_paul': {'en': 'Conversion of St Paul', 'text': 'This feast recalls the moment, told in the Acts of the Apostles, when Saul of Tarsus, a persecutor of the first Christians, was struck down by a vision of the risen Christ on the road to Damascus. Baptised, he became Paul, the Apostle to the Gentiles, whose letters form a large part of the New Testament. The feast closes the annual Week of Prayer for Christian Unity.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conversion_of_Paul_the_Apostle']},
    's_cornelius_and_cyprian': {'en': 'Sts Cornelius and Cyprian', 'text': "Cornelius, bishop of Rome (251–253), and Cyprian, bishop of Carthage (c. 248–258), worked together in the dispute over how to readmit Christians who had lapsed during the persecution of Decius. Cornelius died in exile and Cyprian was beheaded at Carthage in 258; Cyprian's treatise On the Unity of the Church was much read. Both are named in the Roman Canon of the Mass.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyprian']},
    's_cosmas_and_damian': {'en': 'Sts Cosmas and Damian', 'text': 'Cosmas and Damian were, by tradition, twin brothers and physicians from Arabia who practised in Syria and Cilicia and treated the sick without payment, so the East calls them the Holy Unmercenaries. They were martyred probably under Diocletian, and their cult spread from Cyrrhus in Syria across the Christian world. They are patrons of physicians, surgeons and pharmacists and are named in the Roman Canon of the Mass.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosmas_and_Damian']},
    's_cyricus_and_julitta': {'en': 'Sts Kirkos and Iyaluta (Cyricus and Julitta)', 'text': 'Cyricus (Kirkos), a child of three, and his mother Julitta (Iyaluta) were, according to tradition, martyred at Tarsus under Diocletian. In Ethiopian tradition the archangel Gabriel rescued them from a cauldron of boiling liquid, a scene often painted in churches. The 15th of every Ethiopian month is dedicated to them.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quiricus_and_Julietta', 'https://www.eotc-ma.com/beliefs-and-origins-order-of-calend', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Hamle#Hamle19']},
    's_cyril_and_methodius': {'en': 'Sts Cyril and Methodius', 'text': 'Cyril (Constantine, 827–869) and his brother Methodius (815–885), Greeks from Thessalonica, were sent in 863 as missionaries to Great Moravia. They translated the Scriptures and the liturgy into Old Church Slavonic, for which the Glagolitic alphabet was devised, and so laid the foundation of Slavic Christian culture. Honoured as Apostles to the Slavs and Equals to the Apostles, they are also co-patrons of Europe.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyril_and_Methodius']},
    's_cyril_of_alexandria': {'en': 'St Cyril of Alexandria', 'text': 'Patriarch of Alexandria (412-444) who led the defence of the title Theotokos (Mother of God) against Nestorius and presided at the Council of Ephesus in 431. His formula of the one incarnate nature of God the Word became a touchstone of Christology, and his writings are claimed as authoritative by Chalcedonian and non-Chalcedonian churches alike.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyril_of_Alexandria', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/11_3']},
    's_david_builder': {'en': 'St David the Builder', 'text': 'King David IV of Georgia (r. 1089-1125) who reunited the country, recovered Tbilisi and won the battle of Didgori in 1121. He founded the Gelati monastery and academy and reformed the Church; the Georgian Church honours him as a righteous king.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_IV']},
    's_demetrius': {'en': 'St Demetrius of Thessalonica', 'text': 'Christian deacon or soldier of Thessalonica who, tradition holds, was speared to death in prison under Galerius around 306. As patron and defender of Thessalonica he became one of the great soldier-saints of the East, called the Myrrh-streamer because his tomb was said to give off fragrant oil.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demetrius_of_Thessaloniki']},
    's_demetrius_basarabov': {'en': 'St Demetrius the New of Basarabov', 'text': 'Shepherd who became a hermit in a cave by the Lom river near Basarbovo in Bulgaria in the 13th century. His relics were brought to Bucharest in 1774 and rest in the Patriarchal Cathedral; he is patron of the city of Bucharest, and his feast draws large crowds there.', 'sources': ['https://www.crestinortodox.ro/calendar-ortodox/2026-octombrie/']},
    's_demiana': {'en': 'St Demiana', 'text': 'Demiana was, according to Coptic tradition, the daughter of a governor in the northern Delta who lived as a consecrated virgin with forty companions. When her father yielded to the Diocletianic persecution she urged him to return to the faith, and she and her companions were martyred. Her convent near Bilqas is a major Coptic pilgrimage site.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demiana', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/5_13', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/9_12']},
    's_dionysios_of_zakynthos': {'en': 'St Dionysios of Zakynthos', 'text': 'Archbishop of Aegina (1547-1622) and patron of the island of Zakynthos, remembered above all for hiding and forgiving the man who had murdered his brother. His incorrupt relics are kept in the church bearing his name in Zakynthos town.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dionysios_of_Zakynthos']},
    's_dioscorus': {'en': 'St Dioscorus of Alexandria', 'text': "Dioscorus I was the 25th pope of Alexandria (444-454). He was deposed and exiled at the Council of Chalcedon in 451, where he upheld Cyril's formula of one incarnate nature. The Coptic Orthodox Church venerates him as a confessor and champion of its faith; he died in exile at Gangra.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dioscorus_I_of_Alexandria', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/1_7', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Meskerem#Meskerem7']},
    's_dominic': {'en': 'St Dominic', 'text': 'Dominic de Guzmán (1170–1221), a Castilian priest, preached among the Cathars of southern France and founded the Order of Preachers (Dominicans) in 1216, combining study, poverty and preaching. The order spread rapidly to the new universities of Europe. Tradition associates him with the spread of the Rosary.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dominic_de_Guzmán']},
    's_elijah_prophet': {'en': 'St Elijah the Prophet', 'text': 'Elijah the Tishbite was a prophet of the northern kingdom of Israel in the 9th century BC who opposed the worship of Baal under King Ahab. According to the Books of Kings he was carried up into heaven in a chariot of fire, and he appears with Moses at the Transfiguration. He is a model for monks and hermits and a much-loved patron in the Middle East.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elijah']},
    's_elizabeth_of_hungary': {'en': 'St Elizabeth of Hungary', 'text': 'Elizabeth (1207–1231), daughter of King Andrew II of Hungary, married Louis IV, Landgrave of Thuringia, and used her wealth to feed the hungry and build a hospital at the foot of the Wartburg. Widowed at twenty, she became a Franciscan tertiary and spent her remaining years serving the sick at Marburg. Tradition holds that bread she carried to the poor turned into roses.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_of_Hungary']},
    's_ephrem': {'en': 'St Ephrem the Syrian', 'text': "Ephrem (c. 306–373) was a deacon, teacher and poet of Nisibis and later Edessa in Mesopotamia. His hundreds of hymns and verse homilies in Syriac, teaching doctrine through poetry and images, earned him the title 'Harp of the Holy Spirit'. He is honoured by all the Syriac Churches and by the Byzantine tradition, and in 1920 he was declared a Doctor of the Church.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ephrem_the_Syrian']},
    's_epiphanius_of_salamis': {'en': 'St Epiphanius of Cyprus', 'text': 'Palestinian-born monk (c. 310-403) who became Bishop of Salamis (Constantia) and metropolitan of Cyprus. His Panarion, a catalogue of eighty heresies, is a major source for early church history.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epiphanius_of_Salamis']},
    's_euphemia': {'en': 'St Euphemia the All-praised', 'text': 'Young martyr of Chalcedon who, tradition holds, died in the arena around 303 under Diocletian. The Fourth Ecumenical Council met in her basilica at Chalcedon in 451, and Byzantine tradition holds that the Orthodox definition placed in her reliquary was found in her hand; her relics are kept at the Ecumenical Patriarchate.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euphemia']},
    's_euphrasia': {'en': 'St Euphrasia', 'text': "Euphrasia Eluvathingal (1877-1952) was a Syro-Malabar nun of the Congregation of the Mother of Carmel who spent most of her life at the convent of Ollur, near Thrissur. Known locally as the 'praying mother' for her constant prayer, she was canonised in 2014.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euphrasia_Eluvathingal']},
    's_euthymius_great': {'en': 'St Euthymius the Great', 'text': 'Armenian-born monk (377-473) who founded lavras in the Judaean desert and became a spiritual father of Palestinian monasticism. Tradition holds that he brought many Arab nomads to baptism and that his support for the Council of Chalcedon won over the empress Eudocia; St Sabbas was among his disciples.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euthymius_the_Great']},
    's_ewostatewos': {'en': 'St Ewostatewos', 'text': 'Ewostatewos (1273-1352) was a monastic leader of northern Ethiopia and Eritrea who insisted on keeping the Saturday Sabbath as well as Sunday. He went into exile and died in Armenia, but his followers, the Ewostathians, founded influential monasteries such as Debre Bizen, and his position was later accepted by the Church. The 18th of every Ethiopian month is dedicated to him.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ewostatewos', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Meskerem#Meskerem18']},
    's_fathers_first_council': {'en': 'Holy Fathers of the First Ecumenical Council', 'text': "Commemoration of the 318 bishops who met at Nicaea in 325, condemned the teaching of Arius and formulated the first part of the Creed confessing the Son 'of one essence with the Father'. The Byzantine rite honours them on the Sunday after the Ascension.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Council_of_Nicaea']},
    's_fathers_fourth_council': {'en': 'Holy Fathers of the Fourth Ecumenical Council', 'text': 'Commemoration of the 630 bishops of the Council of Chalcedon (451), which taught that Christ is one person in two natures, divine and human, without confusion or separation. In Slavic usage the same Sunday is kept for the Fathers of the first six councils.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Council_of_Chalcedon']},
    's_fathers_seventh_council': {'en': 'Holy Fathers of the Seventh Ecumenical Council', 'text': 'Commemoration of the bishops of the Second Council of Nicaea (787), which ended the first period of iconoclasm and affirmed that holy icons may be venerated because the Word truly became flesh. The Byzantine rite honours them on a Sunday in mid-October.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Council_of_Nicaea']},
    's_faustina_kowalska': {'en': 'St Faustina Kowalska', 'text': "Helena (Faustina) Kowalska (1905–1938) was a Polish nun of the Congregation of Our Lady of Mercy who recorded in her Diary visions of Jesus calling for trust in his Divine Mercy. The image 'Jesus, I trust in you' and the Chaplet of Divine Mercy spread worldwide from her revelations. She was canonised in 2000, when Divine Mercy Sunday was established, and added to the General Roman Calendar in 2020.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faustina_Kowalska']},
    's_filipos_of_debre_bizen': {'en': 'St Filipos of Debre Bizen', 'text': 'Abba Filipos was a monk of the Ewostathian movement, a disciple of Abba Absadi, and the founder of the monastery of Debre Bizen near Nefasit in Eritrea in the late fourteenth century. He defended the observance of the Saturday Sabbath before the royal court. Debre Bizen remains one of the chief monasteries of the Eritrean Church.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Debre_Bizen', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Nehasse#Nehasse5']},
    's_forty_martyrs_of_sebaste': {'en': 'The Forty Martyrs of Sebaste', 'text': 'The Forty Martyrs were Roman soldiers of the Twelfth Legion who, tradition holds, were left to freeze on a frozen lake at Sebaste in Lesser Armenia around AD 320 for refusing to renounce Christ under Licinius. When one of them gave up, a guard who had witnessed the scene took his place, keeping their number at forty. Their story was preached by Basil the Great and is honoured in almost every ancient church.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forty_Martyrs_of_Sebaste']},
    's_forty_nine_martyrs_of_scetis': {'en': 'The Forty-Nine Martyrs of Scetis', 'text': 'The Forty-Nine Elders were monks of Scetis killed by Berber raiders in the fifth century, together with a visiting envoy and his son, who chose to die with them. Their relics are kept at the Monastery of St Macarius. They are among the best-known martyrs of Coptic monasticism.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forty-Nine_Martyrs_of_Scetis', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/5_26', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Tir#Tir26']},
    's_four_evangelists': {'en': 'The Four Evangelists', 'text': "Matthew, Mark, Luke and John are the traditional authors of the four Gospels, written in the second half of the 1st century. Their joint commemoration honours the written witness to the life, death and resurrection of Jesus on which the Church's preaching rests. In Christian art they are represented by the man or angel, the lion, the ox and the eagle.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_Evangelists']},
    's_francis_de_sales': {'en': 'St Francis de Sales', 'text': 'Francis de Sales (1567–1622) was bishop of Geneva, working from Annecy in Savoy during the Catholic Reformation. His Introduction to the Devout Life taught that holiness is possible in every state of life, not only in the cloister. With St Jane Frances de Chantal he founded the Visitation nuns; he is a Doctor of the Church and patron of writers and journalists.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_de_Sales']},
    's_francis_of_assisi': {'en': 'St Francis of Assisi', 'text': 'Francis (c. 1181–1226), son of a cloth merchant of Assisi, renounced his inheritance to live in radical poverty and preach the Gospel, founding the Friars Minor (Franciscans) and, with St Clare, the Poor Clares. He received the stigmata in 1224 and wrote the Canticle of the Creatures. He is patron of Italy and of ecology, and animals are blessed on or near his day.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_of_Assisi']},
    's_francis_xavier': {'en': 'St Francis Xavier', 'text': 'Francis Xavier (1506–1552), a Navarrese nobleman, was one of the first companions of St Ignatius of Loyola and a founding member of the Jesuits. Sent to Asia in 1541, he preached in India, the Malay Archipelago and Japan, and died on an island off China while trying to enter the country. His body lies in Old Goa; he is patron of missions.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Xavier']},
    's_frumentius': {'en': 'St Frumentius (Abba Salama)', 'text': "Frumentius was a Syrian Christian from Tyre who, as a youth, was shipwrecked or captured on the Red Sea coast and brought to the court of Aksum in the fourth century. Consecrated bishop by Athanasius of Alexandria, he led the conversion of the Aksumite kingdom under King Ezana. Ethiopians call him Abba Salama Kesate Berhan, 'Father of Peace, Revealer of Light'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frumentius', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Tahisas#Tahisas18', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Hamle#Hamle26']},
    's_gabra_manfas_qeddus': {'en': 'St Gabra Manfas Qeddus (Abo)', 'text': 'Gabra Manfas Qeddus, popularly called Abo, is one of the most beloved Ethiopian saints; tradition holds he came from Egypt, lived an extraordinarily long ascetic life, and dwelt among lions and leopards on Mount Zuqualla. He is shown covered in hair and surrounded by wild beasts. The 5th of every Ethiopian month is dedicated to him.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gebre_Menfes_Kidus', 'https://eotcmk.org/e/the-feast-of-abune-gebre-menfes-qidus/']},
    's_gabriel_archangel': {'en': 'St Gabriel the Archangel', 'text': "The archangel who in Scripture interprets Daniel's visions, announces the birth of John the Baptist to Zechariah and brings the Annunciation to the Virgin Mary. Eastern churches keep a synaxis in his honour the day after the Annunciation, and he is depicted with Michael on the deacons' doors of the icon screen.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabriel', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/4_22']},
    's_gabriel_of_bialystok': {'en': 'St Gabriel of Białystok (of Zabłudów)', 'text': 'Six-year-old boy from Zwierki near Zabłudów in Podlasie who was abducted and killed in 1690. Healings at his grave led to his veneration as a child-martyr; he was canonised in 1820, his relics have rested in Białystok since 1992, and he is patron of Orthodox youth in Poland.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabriel_of_Białystok']},
    's_gabriel_of_qartmin': {'en': 'St Gabriel of Qartmin', 'text': 'Gabriel (c. 574-648) was abbot of the monastery of Qartmin and bishop of Tur Abdin in south-eastern Turkey. Remembered for his asceticism and miracles, he became the patron of the monastery, which is now called Mor Gabriel. Founded in 397, it is one of the oldest functioning monasteries in the world and the seat of the Syriac Orthodox metropolitan of Tur Abdin.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabriel_of_Beth_Qustan', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mor_Gabriel_Monastery']},
    's_garima': {'en': 'St Garima (Isaac)', 'text': 'Abba Garima, also called Isaac, was one of the Nine Saints; tradition holds he was a prince of the Roman East who became a monk and came to Ethiopia. He founded the monastery near Adwa that keeps the Garima Gospels, among the oldest surviving illustrated Christian gospel books. The 17th of every Ethiopian month is associated with him.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abba_Garima', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Senne#Senne17']},
    's_gayane_and_companions': {'en': 'St Gayane and her Companions', 'text': 'Gayane was, according to Armenian tradition, the abbess of the community of virgins who fled with Hripsime from Rome and was martyred with her nuns at Vagharshapat around AD 301. She and her companions are counted among the first martyrs whose deaths led to the conversion of Armenia. The church of St Gayane near the Mother See of Etchmiadzin marks the site of her martyrdom.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hripsime', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Gayane_Church']},
    's_george': {'en': 'St George', 'text': 'George was a soldier martyred at Lydda (Lod) in Palestine, probably under Diocletian around 303, and was venerated there from early times. He is one of the most popular saints of both East and West, called the Great-martyr and Trophy-bearer, and patron of soldiers and of many lands and cities, among them England, Georgia and Moscow. Tradition, spread in the Middle Ages, holds that he slew a dragon, the image by which he is usually shown.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_George', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/8_23']},
    's_ghevond_priests': {'en': 'Sts Ghevond and the Priests (Ghevondyants)', 'text': 'The Ghevondyants (Leontine martyrs) are the priest Ghevond of Vanand and his fellow clergy who encouraged the Armenian army before the Battle of Avarayr in 451 and were executed in Persian captivity a few years later, in the mid-450s. Their day falls two days before Vardanants and has become the feast of the Armenian clergy, when priests are honoured and church awards are often conferred.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leontine_martyrs', 'https://armenianchurch.us/catholicos-honors-seven-diocesan-clergymen-on-ghevondiantz-day/']},
    's_gorazd_of_prague': {'en': 'St Gorazd of Prague', 'text': 'Matěj Pavlík (1879-1942), a Czech former Catholic priest who became the first bishop of the restored Orthodox Church in Czechoslovakia. He was executed by the Nazis after the paratroopers who killed Reinhard Heydrich were sheltered in his Prague cathedral of Sts Cyril and Methodius; he was glorified as a new martyr in 1987.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gorazd_Pavlík']},
    's_greek_doctors': {'en': 'The Greek Doctors', 'text': "On this Friday the East Syriac churches honour the Greek-speaking Fathers whose teaching they received in Syriac translation. The Assyrian Church of the East names among them in particular Diodore of Tarsus, Theodore of Mopsuestia and Nestorius, while the Catholic Syro-Malabar and Chaldean calendars speak more generally of the Greek Fathers or Doctors. The day expresses the Syriac churches' gratitude for the shared theological inheritance of the early Church.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Syriac_Rite', 'https://www.syromalabarliturgy.org/assets/uploads/pdfs/beef9-2025-2026-panchangam-english-pdf-text-.pdf']},
    's_gregorios_abdul_jaleel': {'en': 'St Gregorios Abdul Jaleel', 'text': 'Gregorios Abdul Jaleel was the Syriac Orthodox metropolitan of Jerusalem who came to Malabar in 1665 at the request of the Saint Thomas Christians and helped restore their links with the Patriarchate of Antioch. He died at North Paravur in Kerala (in 1671 or 1681, according to different sources) and is buried in St Thomas Church there, where his feast draws many pilgrims.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregorios_Abdal_Jaleel', 'http://syriacorthodoxresources.org/Calendar/2014.html']},
    's_gregorios_of_parumala': {'en': 'St Gregorios of Parumala', 'text': 'Geevarghese Mar Gregorios (1848-1902), known as Parumala Thirumeni, was a Malankara metropolitan noted for his asceticism, his charity and his work for education and mission. He died at Parumala, Kerala, where his tomb draws large pilgrimages. In 1947 he became the first canonised saint of Indian origin, and he is honoured by both the Malankara Orthodox and Syriac Orthodox (Jacobite) churches.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregorios_of_Parumala', 'https://mosc.in/saints/st-gregorios-of-parumala-metropolitan-geevarghese-mar-gregorios/']},
    's_gregory_illuminator': {'en': 'St Gregory the Illuminator', 'text': 'Gregory the Illuminator (c. 257-331) was the apostle of Armenia. Tradition holds that King Tiridates III imprisoned him for some thirteen years in a deep pit at Khor Virap, after which Gregory healed the king and baptised him, and Armenia became the first state to adopt Christianity, traditionally in 301. He was the first Catholicos of the Armenian Church, which is sometimes called the Gregorian Church after him.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregory_the_Illuminator', 'https://www.stjohnarmenianchurch.org/st-gregorys-commitment-to-the-pit/']},
    's_gregory_nazianzen': {'en': 'St Gregory Nazianzen', 'text': "Gregory of Nazianzus (c. 329–390), a Cappadocian bishop and friend of St Basil, briefly led the Church of Constantinople and presided for a time over the Council of 381. His five Theological Orations on the Trinity earned him the title 'the Theologian' in the East, and he is also counted among the Doctors of the Church in the West. He wrote much poetry and is honoured as a master of Christian rhetoric.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregory_of_Nazianzus']},
    's_gregory_of_narek': {'en': 'St Gregory of Narek', 'text': "Gregory of Narek (c. 951–1003) was an Armenian monk, priest and mystical poet at the monastery of Narek near Lake Van. His Book of Lamentations, ninety-five prayers 'from the depths of the heart', is a classic of Armenian literature and devotion. Pope Francis declared him a Doctor of the Church in 2015, and his optional memorial was added to the General Roman Calendar in 2021.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregory_of_Narek']},
    's_gregory_of_nyssa': {'en': 'St Gregory of Nyssa', 'text': "Bishop of Nyssa (c. 335-c. 395), younger brother of Basil the Great and one of the three Cappadocian Fathers. A bold speculative theologian, he helped shape the Trinitarian faith confirmed at the Council of Constantinople in 381 and wrote influential works on the soul's endless ascent toward God, such as the Life of Moses.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregory_of_Nyssa']},
    's_gregory_palamas': {'en': 'St Gregory Palamas', 'text': "Athonite monk and Archbishop of Thessalonica (1296-1359) who defended the hesychasts and their prayer of the heart, teaching that God's uncreated energies can truly be known while his essence remains beyond reach. Councils of 1341-1351 upheld his teaching, and the Byzantine rite keeps the second Sunday of Great Lent in his honour as a 'second Sunday of Orthodoxy'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregory_Palamas']},
    's_gregory_the_great': {'en': 'St Gregory the Great', 'text': "Gregory (c. 540–604), a Roman prefect turned monk, became Pope in 590 and governed Rome through plague, famine and Lombard invasion. He sent Augustine of Canterbury to evangelise the English, wrote the Pastoral Rule and the Dialogues, and gave his name to Gregorian chant. Called 'the Dialogist' in the East, he is a Doctor of the Church; 3 September is the day of his consecration as Pope.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Gregory_I']},
    's_guardian_angels': {'en': 'Holy Guardian Angels', 'text': "This memorial honours the angels whom, according to Christian belief rooted in Scripture (e.g. Matthew 18:10), God gives to guard and guide each person. Devotion to guardian angels grew in the late Middle Ages and was promoted by the Franciscans and others; Pope Clement X fixed the feast for the whole Latin Church on 2 October in 1670. Children often learn the prayer 'Angel of God, my guardian dear'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guardian_angel']},
    's_haralambos': {'en': 'St Haralambos (Charalampos)', 'text': 'Priest of Magnesia in Asia Minor who, tradition holds, was martyred at a very great age under Septimius Severus around 202. He is invoked against plague and sickness, and in Bulgaria honey is blessed in church on his feast.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charalambos']},
    's_herman_of_alaska': {'en': 'St Herman of Alaska', 'text': 'Russian monk (c. 1756-1837) of the Valaam monastery who came to Alaska with the mission of 1794 and lived for decades on Spruce Island, teaching and protecting the native Alutiiq people. Glorified in 1970, he was the first saint of the Orthodox Church in America and is called Wonderworker of All America.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herman_of_Alaska']},
    's_hildegard_of_bingen': {'en': 'St Hildegard of Bingen', 'text': 'Hildegard (1098–1179) was a German Benedictine abbess who founded convents at Rupertsberg near Bingen and Eibingen. She recorded her visions in Scivias, composed liturgical music and a sung morality play, wrote on medicine and natural science, and corresponded with popes and emperors. Pope Benedict XVI declared her a Doctor of the Church in 2012, and her optional memorial was added to the General Roman Calendar in 2021.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hildegard_of_Bingen']},
    's_holy_confessors': {'en': 'The Holy Confessors (Friday of Confessors)', 'text': 'On the Friday after Easter the Syriac churches commemorate all the confessors and martyrs who bore witness to the risen Christ. In the Church of the East the day recalls in particular the great persecution of Christians in Persia under Shapur II, which began in the 340s, and it is kept as an all-saints day. The Syro-Malabar Church calls it the feast of the Martyrs or of All Saints.', 'sources': ['http://syriacorthodoxresources.org/Feast/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shemon_bar_Sabbae']},
    's_holy_innocents': {'en': 'Holy Innocents', 'text': 'The Holy Innocents are the children of Bethlehem whom, according to the Gospel of Matthew, King Herod ordered to be killed in his attempt to destroy the newborn Jesus. Christians have honoured them since early times as the first martyrs for Christ, who died in his place. Their day falls in the season of Christmas, and in some countries it is a day of prayer for children.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Massacre_of_the_Innocents', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/5_3']},
    's_holy_translators': {'en': 'The Holy Translators (Targmanchats)', 'text': 'This October feast honours Mesrop Mashtots and the disciples who translated the Scriptures and the Church Fathers into Armenian in the 5th century, among them Yeghishe, Movses Khorenatsi and David the Invincible Philosopher, together with the later Doctors Gregory of Narek and Nerses Shnorhali. It celebrates Armenian learning and literature as gifts of faith. In Armenia the day is also kept as a festival of culture, schools and books.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holy_Translators', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregory_of_Narek', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nerses_IV_the_Gracious']},
    's_hripsime_and_companions': {'en': 'St Hripsime and her Companions', 'text': "Tradition holds that Hripsime was a Christian virgin who fled Roman persecution with Gayane and a community of nuns and was martyred at Vagharshapat by King Tiridates III around AD 301. Their martyrdom is linked in Armenian tradition to the king's madness, his healing by Gregory the Illuminator and the conversion of Armenia. The 7th-century church of St Hripsime at Etchmiadzin stands over her tomb.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hripsime', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Hripsime_Church']},
    's_ignatius_elias_iii': {'en': 'St Ignatius Elias III', 'text': 'Ignatius Elias III (1867-1932) was Syriac Orthodox Patriarch of Antioch from 1917. He travelled to India to make peace within the Malankara Church and died there at Manjinikkara, near Omallur in Kerala, where his tomb became a major pilgrimage centre. The Syriac Orthodox Church declared him a saint in 1987.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ignatius_Elias_III', 'http://syriacorthodoxresources.org/Feast/']},
    's_ignatius_of_antioch': {'en': 'St Ignatius of Antioch', 'text': "Ignatius (died c. 107–110) was an early bishop of Antioch who was taken under guard to Rome to be thrown to the beasts. On the journey he wrote seven letters to churches, which stress unity around the bishop and the reality of Christ's flesh in the Eucharist, and contain the earliest known use of the phrase 'Catholic Church'. He is honoured as an Apostolic Father by Churches of East and West and is called Theophoros, 'God-bearer'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ignatius_of_Antioch']},
    's_ignatius_of_loyola': {'en': 'St Ignatius of Loyola', 'text': "Ignatius of Loyola (1491–1556) was a Basque soldier who, recovering from a war wound, turned to God and wrote the Spiritual Exercises, a method of prayer and discernment still widely used. With companions from the University of Paris he founded the Society of Jesus (Jesuits) in 1540, which became central to Catholic education and missions. His motto was 'For the greater glory of God'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ignatius_of_Loyola']},
    's_innocent_of_alaska': {'en': 'St Innocent of Alaska', 'text': 'Priest John Veniaminov (1797-1879) who served as a missionary in the Aleutians and Alaska, learned the Unangan language and translated the Gospel and liturgy into it. He became bishop of Kamchatka, the Kurils and the Aleutians and finally Metropolitan of Moscow; he is honoured as Enlightener of the Aleuts and Apostle to America.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Innocent_of_Alaska']},
    's_irenaeus': {'en': 'St Irenaeus', 'text': "Irenaeus (c. 130–c. 202), from Smyrna in Asia Minor, heard St Polycarp in his youth and became bishop of Lyon in Gaul. His work Against Heresies refuted the Gnostic teachers and set out the rule of faith handed down through the apostolic succession of bishops. Pope Francis declared him a Doctor of the Church in 2022 with the title 'Doctor of Unity'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irenaeus']},
    's_isaac_the_syrian': {'en': 'St Isaac the Syrian', 'text': 'Isaac of Nineveh was a 7th-century monk of the Church of the East, briefly bishop of Nineveh, who withdrew to the mountains of Beth Huzaye to live as a hermit. His ascetic homilies on prayer, humility and the mercy of God were translated into Greek and other languages and are read by monks of almost every Eastern tradition.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isaac_of_Nineveh']},
    's_jacob_baradaeus': {'en': 'St Jacob Baradaeus', 'text': "Jacob Baradaeus (c. 500-578), bishop of Edessa, travelled across the Roman East disguised as a beggar in a horse-blanket ('burdeono'), ordaining clergy for the communities that rejected the Council of Chalcedon. His work rebuilt their hierarchy, and opponents named the Syriac Orthodox 'Jacobites' after him. He is honoured as one of the founders of the Syriac Orthodox Church.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob_Baradaeus']},
    's_jacob_of_nisibis': {'en': 'St Jacob of Nisibis', 'text': "Jacob (Hakob, Yaqub) was the first bishop of Nisibis in Mesopotamia and died in 338. He took part in the Council of Nicaea in 325 and was the teacher of St Ephrem, and tradition credits his prayers with defending Nisibis during the Persian sieges. The Armenian Church also tells how he climbed Mount Ararat in search of Noah's Ark and was given a fragment of it by an angel.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob_of_Nisibis']},
    's_jacob_of_serugh': {'en': 'St Jacob of Serugh', 'text': "Jacob of Serugh (c. 451-521) was a Syriac poet-theologian and, late in life, bishop of Batnan da-Serugh. He wrote hundreds of metrical homilies (memre) on the Scriptures and the saints, which are still used in Syriac worship. Syriac tradition calls him 'the Flute of the Holy Spirit'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob_of_Serugh']},
    's_james_brother_of_the_lord': {'en': 'St James, brother of the Lord', 'text': "Leader of the church of Jerusalem after the Ascension, called 'brother of the Lord' and 'the Just', who spoke decisively at the Apostolic Council (Acts 15). He is traditionally the author of the Epistle of James and was killed in Jerusalem around 62; the ancient Liturgy of St James, still served on his feast, bears his name.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James,_brother_of_Jesus', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/11_18']},
    's_james_son_of_alphaeus': {'en': 'St James, son of Alphaeus', 'text': 'One of the Twelve, named in every list of the apostles. Byzantine tradition distinguishes him from James the brother of the Lord and holds that he preached in Gaza and Egypt and was crucified; he is sometimes called the brother of Matthew.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James,_son_of_Alphaeus', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/6_10', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Yekatit#Yekatit10']},
    's_james_son_of_zebedee': {'en': 'St James, son of Zebedee', 'text': 'James, son of Zebedee and brother of John, was a Galilean fisherman and one of the Twelve, present with Peter and John at the Transfiguration and in Gethsemane. Beheaded by Herod Agrippa around 44, he was the first apostle to die as a martyr. A medieval tradition holds that his body lies at Santiago de Compostela in Spain, the goal of the great pilgrimage route, the Camino; he is called James the Great.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_the_Great', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James,_son_of_Zebedee', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/8_17']},
    's_jerome': {'en': 'St Jerome', 'text': 'Jerome (c. 347–420), born at Stridon in Dalmatia, was a scholar who served as secretary to Pope Damasus and spent his last decades in a monastery at Bethlehem. His Latin translation of the Bible from the Hebrew and Greek, the Vulgate, became the standard Bible of the Western Church for more than a thousand years. He is a Doctor of the Church and patron of translators, and he is honoured in the East as well.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jerome']},
    's_john_apostle': {'en': 'St John the Apostle', 'text': "John, son of Zebedee and brother of James, was one of the Twelve and is traditionally identified with the 'disciple whom Jesus loved', who stood by the cross and was entrusted with the care of Mary. The Gospel, three Letters and the Book of Revelation are attributed to him. Tradition holds that he lived at Ephesus and died there in old age; the East calls him the Theologian, and his symbol is the eagle.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_the_Apostle', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/5_4']},
    's_john_baptist': {'en': 'St John the Baptist (the Forerunner)', 'text': 'The prophet who prepared the way for Christ, preached repentance by the Jordan and baptised Jesus; he was beheaded at the order of Herod Antipas. Eastern Christians call him the Forerunner (Prodromos) and give him the place beside Christ on the icon screen opposite the Theotokos. Besides his nativity and beheading, the Church keeps a synaxis in his honour the day after Theophany and feasts of the finding of his head.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_the_Baptist']},
    's_john_baptist_de_la_salle': {'en': 'St John Baptist de La Salle', 'text': 'John Baptist de La Salle (1651–1719), a priest of Reims, gave up wealth and position to found the Brothers of the Christian Schools, who taught poor children free of charge. He pioneered teaching in the vernacular, classroom instruction in groups and the training of teachers in colleges. He is patron of teachers.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Baptiste_de_La_Salle']},
    's_john_bosco': {'en': 'St John Bosco', 'text': "John Bosco (1815–1888) was a priest in Turin who gathered poor and abandoned boys of the industrial city, giving them schooling, trades and religious formation. His 'preventive system' of education relied on reason, religion and kindness rather than punishment. He founded the Salesians, named after St Francis de Sales, and with St Mary Mazzarello the Daughters of Mary Help of Christians.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Bosco']},
    's_john_chrysostom': {'en': 'St John Chrysostom', 'text': "John (c. 347–407), a priest of Antioch, became archbishop of Constantinople in 398 and was famed for preaching that earned him the name Chrysostom, 'golden-mouthed'. His criticism of the court and the wealthy led to two exiles, and he died on the road in Pontus. His homilies on Scripture are among the most read works of the Church Fathers; the Divine Liturgy most used in the Byzantine rite bears his name, and he is a Doctor of the Church.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Chrysostom', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/3_17']},
    's_john_climacus': {'en': 'St John Climacus (of the Ladder)', 'text': 'Abbot of the monastery of Mount Sinai in the 7th century, author of The Ladder of Divine Ascent, which describes the spiritual life as thirty rungs from renunciation to love. The book is read in Orthodox monasteries during Great Lent, and its author is honoured on the fourth Sunday of the fast.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Climacus']},
    's_john_damascene': {'en': 'St John Damascene', 'text': 'John of Damascus (c. 675–749), from a Christian Arab family that served the caliphs, became a monk at the Lavra of St Sabbas near Jerusalem. He defended the veneration of icons against the iconoclasts and summed up Greek patristic teaching in The Fount of Knowledge. His hymns are sung in the Byzantine liturgy, and he is a Doctor of the Church.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_Damascus']},
    's_john_fisher_and_thomas_more': {'en': 'Sts John Fisher and Thomas More', 'text': 'John Fisher (1469–1535), bishop of Rochester and cardinal, and Thomas More (1478–1535), lawyer, humanist author of Utopia and former Lord Chancellor of England, refused to accept King Henry VIII as Supreme Head of the Church in England. Both were beheaded in 1535. They were canonised together in 1935; More is patron of statesmen and politicians.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_More']},
    's_john_henry_newman': {'en': 'St John Henry Newman', 'text': 'John Henry Newman (1801–1890) was an Anglican priest and Oxford scholar, a leader of the Oxford Movement, who was received into the Catholic Church on 9 October 1845. He founded the Birmingham Oratory, wrote the Apologia pro Vita Sua, The Idea of a University and The Dream of Gerontius, and was made a cardinal in 1879. Canonised in 2019, he was declared a Doctor of the Church in 2025 and added to the General Roman Calendar in 2026.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Henry_Newman']},
    's_john_maron': {'en': 'St John Maron', 'text': 'John Maron (died 707) is venerated as the first Maronite Patriarch of Antioch, traditionally elected in the late 7th century. He organised the monastic community of St Maron into a church with its own hierarchy, which gave the Maronites their distinct identity. He is honoured as a confessor and founding father of the Maronite Church.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Maron']},
    's_john_of_avila': {'en': 'St John of Ávila', 'text': 'John of Ávila (1500–1569) was a Spanish priest and preacher, called the Apostle of Andalusia, who also founded colleges and advised reformers such as Teresa of Ávila, Ignatius of Loyola and John of God. His spiritual treatise Audi, filia was widely read. Pope Benedict XVI named him a Doctor of the Church in 2012, and he is patron of Spanish diocesan clergy.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_Ávila']},
    's_john_of_damascus': {'en': 'St John of Damascus', 'text': 'Arab Christian monk and priest (c. 675-749) of the Mar Saba monastery near Jerusalem, formerly an official of the Umayyad court. He wrote the classic defence of holy icons against Byzantine iconoclasm, a summary of the Orthodox faith and many hymns, including the Paschal canon still sung today.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_Damascus']},
    's_john_of_kronstadt': {'en': 'St John of Kronstadt', 'text': "Russian priest (1829-1908) who served for over fifty years in the naval town of Kronstadt, celebrating the liturgy daily, founding a 'House of Industry' for the poor and drawing crowds from all Russia. His diary My Life in Christ is a classic of Orthodox devotion.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_Kronstadt']},
    's_john_of_rila': {'en': 'St John of Rila', 'text': 'Bulgarian hermit (c. 876-946) who lived in caves of the Rila mountains and attracted the disciples from whom the Rila Monastery grew. Honoured as heavenly patron of the Bulgarian people, he is the most venerated Bulgarian saint, and his relics rest in the monastery.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_Rila']},
    's_john_of_the_cross': {'en': 'St John of the Cross', 'text': "John of the Cross (1542–1591), a Spanish Carmelite friar and priest, worked with St Teresa of Ávila to reform the Carmelites and was imprisoned by opponents of the reform in Toledo, where he began his poetry. His poems and commentaries, such as The Dark Night and The Spiritual Canticle, describe the soul's journey to union with God. He is a Doctor of the Church and is counted among the great poets of the Spanish language.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_the_Cross']},
    's_john_paul_ii': {'en': 'St John Paul II', 'text': 'Karol Wojtyła (1920–2005), archbishop of Kraków, was elected Pope in 1978, the first non-Italian pope in over four centuries. During one of the longest pontificates he made more than a hundred journeys abroad, supported the Solidarity movement in Poland and founded World Youth Days. He was canonised in 2014; his memorial falls on the anniversary of his inauguration as Pope in 1978.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_John_Paul_II']},
    's_john_the_baptist': {'en': 'St John the Baptist', 'text': 'John, son of Zechariah and Elizabeth, preached repentance by the Jordan and baptised Christ. He was beheaded by Herod Antipas for condemning his unlawful marriage. The Church honours him as the last of the prophets and the Forerunner of Christ.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_the_Baptist', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/1_2', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/10_30', 'https://chaldeanchurch.org/liturgical-year-of-the-chaldean-church/']},
    's_john_the_short': {'en': 'St John the Short', 'text': "John Kolobos ('the Short', c. 339-c. 405) was a monk of Scetis famed for obedience. Tradition holds that his elder told him to water a dry stick daily, and it grew into a fruit-bearing 'tree of obedience'. His sayings are prominent in the Sayings of the Desert Fathers.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_the_Dwarf', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/2_20']},
    's_john_vianney': {'en': 'St John Vianney', 'text': 'Jean-Marie Vianney (1786–1859) struggled with his studies but was ordained and sent to the small village of Ars near Lyon. As its parish priest, the Curé of Ars, he became famous as a confessor, spending up to sixteen hours a day hearing confessions of pilgrims from all over France. He is patron of parish priests.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Vianney']},
    's_josaphat': {'en': 'St Josaphat', 'text': 'Josaphat Kuntsevych (c. 1580–1623) was a Ruthenian monk of the Basilian order and archeparch of Polotsk in the Ukrainian Greek Catholic Church, which had entered into union with Rome at Brest in 1596. He worked for church unity and was killed by opponents of the union at Vitebsk. Canonised in 1867, he is often described as the first Eastern Catholic saint formally canonised by Rome.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josaphat_Kuntsevych']},
    's_josephine_bakhita': {'en': 'St Josephine Bakhita', 'text': 'Bakhita (c. 1869–1947) was born in Darfur, kidnapped as a child by slave traders and sold several times before being brought to Italy. There she became a Christian, won her freedom in court and joined the Canossian Sisters, serving for decades in Schio. Canonised in 2000 as the first saint of Sudan, she is patron of victims of human trafficking, and her day is a world day of prayer against trafficking.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josephine_Bakhita']},
    's_juan_diego': {'en': 'St Juan Diego Cuauhtlatoatzin', 'text': 'Juan Diego Cuauhtlatoatzin (1474–1548) was an Indigenous Nahua man of central Mexico who, according to tradition, saw the Virgin Mary on the hill of Tepeyac in December 1531. His cloak, the tilma bearing the image of Our Lady of Guadalupe, is venerated in her basilica in Mexico City. Canonised in 2002, he was the first Indigenous saint of the Americas.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Diego']},
    's_jude_apostle': {'en': 'St Jude the Apostle, brother of the Lord', 'text': "Apostle called Jude (Thaddeus or Lebbaeus in some lists), traditionally the author of the short Epistle of Jude. Byzantine use honours him as a 'brother of the Lord' and holds that he preached in Mesopotamia and was martyred; in the West he is kept with Simon and is invoked in desperate causes.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jude_the_Apostle']},
    's_justin_martyr': {'en': 'St Justin Martyr', 'text': 'Justin (c. 100–165), a philosopher born at Flavia Neapolis (Nablus) in Samaria, became a Christian after long searching and taught in Rome. His Apologies and Dialogue with Trypho defend the faith to pagans and Jews and include the earliest detailed description of the Sunday Eucharist. He was beheaded in Rome with several of his students and is patron of philosophers.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Justin_Martyr']},
    's_karelian_enlighteners': {'en': 'Synaxis of the Enlighteners of Karelia', 'text': "Joint commemoration of the saints who brought the Christian faith to Karelia and the Finnish borderlands, among them Sergius and Herman of Valaam, Arsenius of Konevets, Alexander of Svir and Tryphon of Pechenga. The Finnish Orthodox Church keeps it on the Saturday of All Saints' weekend in early November.", 'sources': ['https://ort.fi/kirkkokalenteri/20251101/']},
    's_kuriakose_elias_chavara': {'en': 'St Kuriakose Elias Chavara', 'text': 'Kuriakose Elias Chavara (1805-1871) was a Syro-Malabar priest who co-founded the Carmelites of Mary Immaculate, the first Indian religious congregation for men, and a congregation for women. He promoted schools attached to parishes, printing and the renewal of the liturgy in Kerala. He was canonised in 2014.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kuriakose_Elias_Chavara']},
    's_kyrillos_vi': {'en': 'St Kyrillos VI', 'text': "Kyrillos VI (1902-1971) was the 116th pope of Alexandria from 1959, a monk known for celebrating the liturgy daily. He built the new St Mark's Cathedral in Cairo and received relics of St Mark from Rome in 1968. The Coptic Orthodox Holy Synod proclaimed him a saint in 2013.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Cyril_VI_of_Alexandria', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/6_30']},
    's_lalibela': {'en': 'St Lalibela (Gabra Masqal)', 'text': 'Lalibela, crowned Gabra Masqal, was a king of the Zagwe dynasty in the late twelfth and early thirteenth centuries. Tradition credits him with the rock-hewn churches of the town that now bears his name, conceived as a New Jerusalem. He is venerated as a saintly king.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gebre_Mesqel_Lalibela', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Senne#Senne12']},
    's_lawrence': {'en': 'St Lawrence', 'text': "Lawrence was one of the seven deacons of Rome, martyred in 258 in the persecution of Valerian a few days after Pope Sixtus II. Tradition holds that, ordered to hand over the Church's treasures, he presented the poor, and that he was roasted on a gridiron, joking with his executioners. He is one of the most venerated martyrs of Rome; the Perseid meteors of mid-August are popularly called the 'Tears of St Lawrence'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Lawrence']},
    's_lazar_of_serbia': {'en': 'St Prince Lazar and the Martyrs of Kosovo (Vidovdan)', 'text': 'Prince Lazar Hrebeljanović (c. 1329-1389) led the Serbian army against the Ottomans at the Battle of Kosovo, where he and many of his men were killed. The Serbian Church honours him as a great-martyr together with those who fell, and the day, Vidovdan, has become a central feast of Serbian memory.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lazar_of_Serbia', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vidovdan']},
    's_lazarus_of_bethany': {'en': 'St Lazarus of Bethany (the Four-days dead)', 'text': 'Friend of Jesus and brother of Martha and Mary whom Christ raised from the dead after four days (John 11). Tradition holds that he later became the first bishop of Kition (Larnaca) in Cyprus, where the church of St Lazarus stands over his tomb.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lazarus_of_Bethany']},
    's_leo_the_great': {'en': 'St Leo the Great', 'text': 'Leo I (c. 400–461) was Pope from 440. His Tome to Flavian, explaining that Christ is one person in two natures, was acclaimed at the Council of Chalcedon in 451 and became a cornerstone of Christological teaching in the Churches that accept that council. Tradition holds that he met Attila the Hun in 452 and persuaded him to withdraw from Italy, and later limited the sack of Rome by the Vandals. He is a Doctor of the Church.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Leo_I']},
    's_libanos': {'en': 'St Libanos (Mata’e)', 'text': "Abuna Libanos, also called Mata'e, is remembered in Ethiopian and Eritrean tradition as an early missionary monk who came from the Roman East and founded the monastery of Debre Libanos of Ham in Eritrea. Tradition places him in the time of the Nine Saints. He is especially venerated in the Eritrean highlands.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Debre_Libanos_(Eritrea)', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Tir#Tir3']},
    's_lucy': {'en': 'St Lucy', 'text': 'Lucy was a virgin martyr of Syracuse in Sicily, put to death in the persecution of Diocletian around 304. Her name comes from the Latin for light, and she is patron of the blind and of people with eye trouble. She is named in the Roman Canon of the Mass, and in Scandinavia her day is celebrated with processions led by a girl wearing a crown of candles.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Lucy']},
    's_luke': {'en': 'St Luke', 'text': "Luke, described by St Paul as 'the beloved physician', was a companion of Paul on his journeys and is the traditional author of the Gospel of Luke and the Acts of the Apostles. His Gospel gives the fullest account of the infancy of Jesus and many of the best-known parables. Tradition holds that he painted the first icon of the Virgin Mary; his symbol is the ox, and he is patron of physicians and artists.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luke_the_Evangelist', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/2_22']},
    's_macarius_great': {'en': 'St Macarius the Great', 'text': "Egyptian desert father (c. 300-391), a disciple of St Anthony, who founded the monastic settlement of Scetis in the Wadi Natrun. Known as the 'spirit-bearer' for his gentleness and gifts of discernment, he is associated with the sayings of the Desert Fathers and with homilies on the prayer of the heart; the monastery bearing his name still flourishes in Egypt.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macarius_of_Egypt', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/7_27']},
    's_mar_aba_catholicos': {'en': 'St Mar Aba the Great', 'text': 'Mar Aba I was a Zoroastrian convert who became Catholicos of the Church of the East from 540 to 552. A scholar who had studied at Edessa and Alexandria, he reformed church discipline and marriage law and healed a schism in the church. He was exiled under the Persian king Khosrow I and died in 552 after years of confinement.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aba_I']},
    's_mari_apostle': {'en': 'St Mari the Apostle', 'text': 'Mari was, according to tradition, a disciple of Addai who carried the Gospel from Edessa into Mesopotamia and Persia in the 1st century and founded the church of Seleucia-Ctesiphon. The Church of the East honours him as an apostle of the East, and the Liturgy of Addai and Mari, one of the oldest Christian eucharistic prayers, is named after him and his teacher.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Mari', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liturgy_of_Addai_and_Mari']},
    's_mariam_thresia': {'en': 'St Mariam Thresia', 'text': 'Mariam Thresia Chiramel Mankidiyan (1876-1926) was a Syro-Malabar mystic from Puthenchira, Kerala, who founded the Congregation of the Holy Family to serve families, the poor and the sick. She was canonised in 2019.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mariam_Thresia_Chiramel']},
    's_marina': {'en': 'St Marina (Margaret) the Great-martyr', 'text': 'Virgin martyr of Antioch in Pisidia who, tradition holds, was beheaded under Diocletian after defeating a demon in her prison cell. Known as Margaret in the West, she is especially loved in Greece, Cyprus and Lebanon, and many churches and villages there bear her name.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_the_Virgin']},
    's_marina_of_antioch': {'en': 'St Marina of Antioch', 'text': 'Marina, called Margaret in the West, was a virgin martyr of Antioch in Pisidia, traditionally said to have been martyred under Diocletian. Tradition holds that she overcame the devil in the form of a dragon while in prison. She is widely venerated in the East as a great-martyr.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_the_Virgin', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/11_23']},
    's_mark': {'en': 'St Mark', 'text': 'Mark the Evangelist is traditionally identified with John Mark of the Acts of the Apostles, a companion of Paul and Barnabas and later of Peter, whose preaching his Gospel is said to record. His Gospel is generally held to be the earliest of the four. Tradition holds that he founded the Church of Alexandria and was martyred there; his symbol is the winged lion, and Venice, which claims his relics, took him as patron.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_the_Evangelist', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/8_30']},
    's_martha_mary_and_lazarus': {'en': 'Sts Martha, Mary and Lazarus', 'text': "Martha, Mary and their brother Lazarus of Bethany were friends of Jesus who welcomed him into their home; the Gospel of John tells that he raised Lazarus from the tomb. Martha's confession of faith and Mary's listening at Jesus' feet are well-known Gospel scenes. Since 2021 the three siblings are kept together on 29 July in the Roman Calendar, a day formerly given to Martha alone.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martha']},
    's_martin_de_porres': {'en': 'St Martin de Porres', 'text': 'Martin de Porres (1579–1639), born in Lima to a Spanish nobleman and a freed woman of African descent, became a Dominican lay brother. He worked as barber, infirmarian and almoner, caring for the sick and poor of every race and founding an orphanage. Canonised in 1962, he is patron of social justice and of people of mixed race.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martin_de_Porres']},
    's_martin_of_tours': {'en': 'St Martin of Tours', 'text': 'Martin (c. 316–397), born in Pannonia, served in the Roman army; tradition holds that at Amiens he cut his cloak in half to clothe a beggar and then saw Christ in a dream wearing it. Leaving the army, he founded one of the first monasteries in Gaul at Ligugé and became bishop of Tours. One of the first non-martyrs venerated as a saint, he is patron of France and of soldiers, and his day, Martinmas, is marked with lantern processions.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martin_of_Tours']},
    's_mary_magdalene': {'en': 'St Mary Magdalene', 'text': "Mary of Magdala was a Galilean disciple of Jesus who, according to the Gospels, stood by the cross and was the first to see the risen Christ and announce his resurrection to the apostles. She is therefore called 'Apostle to the Apostles' and, in the East, Equal to the Apostles and Myrrh-bearer. Tradition holds that she later lived at Ephesus, while a Western tradition links her to Provence; an Eastern tradition holds that she gave a red egg to the emperor Tiberius.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Magdalene', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/11_28']},
    's_mary_of_egypt': {'en': 'St Mary of Egypt', 'text': 'Mary of Egypt (c. 344-421) was, according to her Life, a woman of Alexandria who lived dissolutely until a conversion in Jerusalem. She then spent decades as a penitent hermit in the desert beyond the Jordan. She is honoured as a model of repentance.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_of_Egypt', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/8_6']},
    's_matrona_of_moscow': {'en': 'St Matrona of Moscow', 'text': 'Blind peasant woman (1881-1952) from the Tula region who, unable to walk from youth, lived in Moscow sheltered by friends and received crowds who came for prayer and counsel through the Soviet years. Glorified in 1999, she is one of the most visited saints in Russia, and her relics rest in the Intercession convent in Moscow.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matrona_Nikonova']},
    's_matthew': {'en': 'St Matthew', 'text': 'Matthew, also called Levi, was a tax collector at Capernaum whom Jesus called to be one of the Twelve Apostles. The first Gospel is traditionally attributed to him and was long considered to have been written for Jewish Christians. Traditions about his later preaching and martyrdom point to Ethiopia, Persia or other lands; his symbol as Evangelist is a winged man.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matthew_the_Apostle', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/2_12']},
    's_matthias': {'en': 'St Matthias', 'text': 'Matthias was chosen by lot, as told in the Acts of the Apostles, to take the place of Judas Iscariot among the Twelve, having followed Jesus from his baptism to the Ascension. Little else is known of his life; traditions place his preaching and martyrdom variously in Judaea, Ethiopia or Colchis. Relics claimed as his are kept at Trier in Germany.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matthias_the_Apostle', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Matthias', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/7_8']},
    's_maxim_sandovich': {'en': 'St Maxim Sandovich (of Gorlice)', 'text': 'Lemko priest (1886-1914) who brought the Orthodox faith back to villages of the Carpathian region of Galicia. Accused of spying for Russia, he was shot without trial by Austro-Hungarian soldiers in Gorlice at the start of the First World War; the Polish Orthodox Church canonised him in 1994.', 'sources': ['https://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maksym_Gorlicki']},
    's_maximilian_kolbe': {'en': 'St Maximilian Kolbe', 'text': 'Maximilian Kolbe (1894–1941) was a Polish Franciscan friar who founded the Militia Immaculatae and a large friary and publishing house at Niepokalanów, and also worked as a missionary in Japan. Imprisoned at Auschwitz, he offered his life in place of a stranger, a family man condemned to death by starvation, and was killed by lethal injection. He was canonised in 1982 as a martyr of charity.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximilian_Kolbe']},
    's_maximus_confessor': {'en': 'St Maximus the Confessor', 'text': 'Byzantine monk and theologian (c. 580-662) who defended the teaching that Christ has a fully human will as well as a divine one. For refusing the imperial Monothelete policy he had his tongue and right hand cut off and died in exile; the Sixth Ecumenical Council (680-681) vindicated him. His writings on deification and the liturgy are central to Orthodox spirituality.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximus_the_Confessor']},
    's_menas': {'en': 'St Menas', 'text': "Menas was an Egyptian soldier in the Roman army who, according to tradition, left the army, confessed his faith and was beheaded about 309. His shrine at Abu Mena near Alexandria became one of the great pilgrimage centres of late antiquity, known for clay flasks of holy oil. He is called 'the Wonderworker'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menas_of_Egypt', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Menas', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/3_15']},
    's_mercurius': {'en': 'St Mercurius', 'text': "Mercurius was a Roman officer martyred at Caesarea in Cappadocia in the mid-third century. Tradition holds that an angel gave him a second sword to defeat the empire's enemies, so Copts call him Abu Sefein, 'father of the two swords', and Ethiopians call him Merkorios. He is venerated in both East and West as a soldier-martyr.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Mercurius', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercurius', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/3_25']},
    's_michael_archangel': {'en': 'St Michael the Archangel', 'text': "Michael, whose Hebrew name means 'Who is like God?', appears in the Books of Daniel and Revelation and in the Letter of Jude as the leader of the angels against the forces of evil. He is honoured in Judaism, Christianity and Islam, and Christians invoke him as protector of the Church and of the dying. He is usually shown in armour with a sword or spear, standing over a dragon or Satan.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_(archangel)', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/3_12']},
    's_monica': {'en': 'St Monica', 'text': 'Monica (c. 332–387), born in Roman North Africa, was the mother of St Augustine of Hippo. As Augustine recounts in his Confessions, she prayed for years for his conversion, followed him to Milan, and died at Ostia soon after his baptism. She is patron of mothers and of those praying for wayward children.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Monica']},
    's_moses_the_black': {'en': 'St Moses the Black', 'text': "Moses (c. 330-405) was, by tradition, an Ethiopian slave turned robber in Egypt who repented and became a monk in Scetis. He was famed for humility and gentleness towards others' faults and was ordained priest. Tradition holds he refused to flee when raiders attacked the monastery and was killed with several companions.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moses_the_Black', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/10_24']},
    's_myrrhbearers': {'en': 'Sunday of the Holy Myrrhbearers', 'text': 'Commemoration of the women who came to the tomb with spices on Easter morning and were the first to hear of the Resurrection, together with Joseph of Arimathea and Nicodemus, who buried Christ. Kept on the third Sunday of Pascha, it is often treated as a day honouring the work of Christian women.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myrrhbearers']},
    's_narsai': {'en': 'St Narsai', 'text': "Narsai (c. 399-502) was a poet and theologian of the Church of the East who led the School of Edessa and, after his expulsion around 471, founded the famous School of Nisibis. His metrical homilies and hymns are classics of East Syriac literature and liturgy, and he was given the title 'Harp of the Spirit'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Narsai']},
    's_nektarios_of_aegina': {'en': 'St Nektarios of Aegina', 'text': 'Greek bishop (1846-1920), Metropolitan of Pentapolis under the Patriarchate of Alexandria, who after being removed from Egypt by intrigues lived in poverty in Greece as a preacher and teacher and founded a convent on Aegina. He was glorified in 1961, is invoked for healing, and his shrine on Aegina is one of the busiest in Greece.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nectarios_of_Aegina']},
    's_nerses_the_great': {'en': 'St Nerses the Great', 'text': 'Nerses I the Great was Catholicos of Armenia in the 4th century (c. 353-373) and a great-great-grandson of Gregory the Illuminator. Educated in Caesarea, he reorganised the Armenian Church, founded hospitals, orphanages and monasteries, and is remembered as the founder of organised Christian charity in Armenia. He was poisoned, tradition holds, at the order of King Pap, whose conduct he had rebuked.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nerses_I', 'https://armenianchurch.us/the-illuminators-sons-grandsons-6/']},
    's_new_martyrs_russia': {'en': 'New Martyrs and Confessors of the Russian Church', 'text': "Commemoration of the Orthodox bishops, clergy, monastics and laity who were killed or persecuted for their faith under Soviet rule after 1917, among them Metropolitan Vladimir of Kyiv and the family of the last tsar. The Russian Church keeps the feast on a Sunday near the date of Metropolitan Vladimir's death in 1918.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Martyrs_and_Confessors_of_the_Russian_Orthodox_Church']},
    's_nicholas': {'en': 'St Nicholas', 'text': 'Nicholas was a fourth-century bishop of Myra in Lycia (now Demre, Turkey), remembered as a defender of the faith and of the wrongly condemned. Tradition holds that he secretly gave dowries to three poor girls, which gave rise to his role as a bringer of gifts and to the figure of Santa Claus. His relics were taken to Bari in 1087; he is patron of children, sailors and Russia, and is called the Wonderworker.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Nicholas', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/4_10']},
    's_nimatullah_kassab': {'en': 'St Nimatullah Kassab Al-Hardini', 'text': 'Nimatullah Youssef Kassab Al-Hardini (1808-1858) was a monk, priest and theologian of the Lebanese Maronite Order, remembered for his life of prayer, obedience and teaching. Among his students was Charbel Makhlouf. He was canonised in 2004.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nimatullah_Kassab']},
    's_nino': {'en': 'St Nino, Enlightener of Georgia', 'text': 'Woman from Cappadocia who, tradition holds, came to Iberia (eastern Georgia) in the early 4th century with a cross of vine branches bound with her own hair and converted King Mirian and Queen Nana. Honoured as Equal-to-the-Apostles, she is the patron of Georgia, and her grapevine cross is the emblem of the Georgian Church.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Nino']},
    's_olga_of_kyiv': {'en': 'St Olga of Kyiv', 'text': "Princess and regent of Kyiv (c. 890-969), widow of Prince Igor, who was baptised in Constantinople around 957 with the name Helen. The first ruler of Rus' to become Christian, she prepared the way for the baptism of her grandson Vladimir and is honoured as Equal-to-the-Apostles.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olga_of_Kiev']},
    's_pachomius': {'en': 'St Pachomius the Great', 'text': 'Egyptian former soldier (c. 292-348) who founded the first communities of monks living under a common rule at Tabennisi in the Thebaid. His rule, with its shared work, prayer and meals, became the model for cenobitic monasticism in East and West, and his foundations grew to thousands of monks and nuns in his lifetime.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachomius_the_Great', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/9_14']},
    's_paisios': {'en': 'St Paisios the Athonite', 'text': 'Greek monk (1924-1994), born in Cappadocia, who lived as an ascetic on Mount Athos and at Sinai and received thousands of visitors seeking counsel. His sayings are widely read, and the Ecumenical Patriarchate glorified him in 2015; his grave is at the Souroti convent near Thessaloniki.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paisios_of_Mount_Athos']},
    's_panteleimon': {'en': 'St Panteleimon the Great-martyr and Healer', 'text': "Physician of Nicomedia who, tradition holds, treated the sick without payment and was martyred under Diocletian in 305. He heads the Eastern list of 'unmercenary' healer-saints and is invoked in the blessing of oil for the sick; the Russian monastery on Mount Athos bears his name.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Pantaleon']},
    's_paraskeva_of_iasi': {'en': 'St Paraskeva of the Balkans (of Iasi, Petka)', 'text': 'Hermitess of the 11th century from Epivates in Thrace who lived as an ascetic near Constantinople and in the desert of the Jordan before returning home to die. Her relics passed through Tarnovo and Belgrade to Iasi in 1641; as Petka she is beloved among Bulgarians and Serbs, and her Iasi feast draws the largest pilgrimage in Romania.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paraskeva_of_the_Balkans']},
    's_paraskevi': {'en': 'St Paraskevi of Rome', 'text': 'Virgin martyr of the second century who, tradition holds, was born to Christian parents on a Friday (Paraskevi in Greek), preached the faith and was beheaded under Antoninus Pius. She is invoked for healing of the eyes, and countless chapels in Greece and the Balkans bear her name.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paraskevi_of_Rome']},
    's_patrick': {'en': 'St Patrick', 'text': 'Patrick was a fifth-century Romano-British Christian who was carried off to Ireland as a slave in his youth, escaped, and later returned as a bishop and missionary. His Confession and Letter to Coroticus are the earliest documents of Irish history. He is honoured as the Apostle and patron of Ireland; tradition holds that he explained the Trinity with a shamrock.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Patrick']},
    's_paul_first_hermit': {'en': 'St Paul the First Hermit', 'text': "Paul of Thebes (c. 227-341) fled to the Egyptian desert during the Decian persecution and lived there in solitude for some ninety years. Jerome's Life records that Anthony visited him shortly before his death and buried him. The Copts call him Anba Paula, and his monastery stands near Anthony's in the Eastern Desert.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_of_Thebes', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/6_2']},
    's_paul_miki_and_companions': {'en': 'Sts Paul Miki and Companions', 'text': 'Paul Miki, a Japanese Jesuit, and twenty-five companions, among them Franciscan friars, catechists and young boys, were crucified on a hill at Nagasaki on 5 February 1597 by order of Toyotomi Hideyoshi. They were the first martyrs of the Church in Japan. Their deaths began some two and a half centuries of persecution, during which hidden Christian communities kept the faith in secret.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/26_Martyrs_of_Japan']},
    's_pavol_gojdic': {'en': 'Bl. Pavol Peter Gojdič', 'text': 'Basilian monk and Greek Catholic Bishop of Prešov (1888-1960) who resisted the suppression of his church by the Czechoslovak communist state in 1950. Sentenced to life imprisonment, he died in Leopoldov prison; he was beatified as a martyr in 2001, and Yad Vashem has recognised him as Righteous Among the Nations.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pavel_Peter_Gojdič']},
    's_perpetua_and_felicity': {'en': 'Sts Perpetua and Felicity', 'text': "Perpetua, a young noblewoman and mother, and Felicity, a slave who gave birth in prison, were martyred with companions in the amphitheatre of Carthage in 203. The Passion of Perpetua and Felicity includes Perpetua's own prison diary, one of the earliest surviving writings by a Christian woman. Both are named in the Roman Canon of the Mass.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perpetua_and_Felicity']},
    's_peter_and_paul': {'en': 'Sts Peter and Paul', 'text': 'Peter, the leader of the Twelve, and Paul, the apostle to the Gentiles, were both martyred in Rome under Nero in the 60s AD according to tradition. They are honoured together as the pillars of the Church. Besides the June feast kept by most churches, the East Syriac tradition commemorates them on the second Friday after Epiphany.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feast_of_Saints_Peter_and_Paul', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Peter', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_the_Apostle']},
    's_peter_seal_of_martyrs': {'en': 'St Peter, Seal of the Martyrs', 'text': "Peter I was the 17th pope of Alexandria (300-311) and led his church through the Great Persecution. He was beheaded in 311, and Coptic tradition holds he was the last bishop martyred by the pagan empire, hence his title 'Seal of the Martyrs'. He is honoured also for his canons on the readmission of the lapsed.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_of_Alexandria', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/3_29']},
    's_philip_apostle': {'en': 'St Philip the Apostle', 'text': "Philip, from Bethsaida in Galilee, was one of the Twelve Apostles; in John's Gospel he brings Nathanael to Jesus and asks him, 'Lord, show us the Father'. Tradition holds that he preached in Asia Minor and died as a martyr at Hierapolis in Phrygia, where his tomb was identified by archaeologists in 2011. He is distinct from Philip the Deacon of the Acts of the Apostles.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_the_Apostle', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/3_18']},
    's_philip_neri': {'en': 'St Philip Neri', 'text': 'Philip Neri (1515–1595), a Florentine who spent his life in Rome, drew clergy and lay people to prayer, Scripture, music and works of charity through informal gatherings that became the Congregation of the Oratory. Known for his humour and gentleness, he is called the Apostle of Rome. The musical oratorio takes its name from the services held in his Oratory.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_Neri']},
    's_pio_of_pietrelcina': {'en': 'St Pius of Pietrelcina (Padre Pio)', 'text': 'Francesco Forgione (1887–1968), known as Padre Pio, was a Capuchin friar of southern Italy who lived for fifty years at San Giovanni Rotondo. He bore the stigmata, spent long hours hearing confessions and founded a large hospital, the Home for the Relief of Suffering. Canonised in 2002, he is one of the most popular saints of modern Italy.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio']},
    's_pishoy': {'en': 'St Pishoy', 'text': "Pishoy (Anba Bishoy, c. 320-417) was a monk of Scetis, a disciple of Pambo and a companion of John the Short. Tradition holds that he washed the feet of a stranger who proved to be Christ, and his body is venerated as incorrupt in the Monastery of St Pishoy at Wadi el-Natrun. The Copts call him 'the perfect man' and 'beloved of our Good Saviour'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pishoy', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/11_8']},
    's_pius_x': {'en': 'St Pius X', 'text': "Giuseppe Sarto (1835–1914), son of a village postman in the Veneto, became Pope Pius X in 1903 with the motto 'to restore all things in Christ'. He encouraged frequent Communion and lowered the age of First Communion to about seven, ordered the codification of canon law and reformed church music and the breviary. He was canonised in 1954.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Pius_X']},
    's_polycarp': {'en': 'St Polycarp', 'text': 'Polycarp (c. 69–155) was bishop of Smyrna in Asia Minor and, by tradition, a disciple of the Apostle John, which makes him a living link between the apostles and later generations. His Letter to the Philippians survives, and the Martyrdom of Polycarp, describing his death by fire in old age, is the oldest detailed account of a Christian martyrdom. He is honoured as a martyr and Apostolic Father by Churches of both East and West.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polycarp']},
    's_porphyrios': {'en': 'St Porphyrios of Kafsokalyvia', 'text': 'Greek monk and priest (1906-1991) of the Kafsokalyvia skete on Mount Athos who served for decades as chaplain of a hospital in Athens and became one of the best-known elders of modern Greece. The Ecumenical Patriarchate glorified him in 2013.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Porphyrios_of_Kafsokalyvia']},
    's_rabban_hormizd': {'en': 'St Rabban Hormizd', 'text': 'Rabban Hormizd was a monk of the Church of the East from Beth Lapat in Persia who, in the 7th century, founded the monastery in the mountains above Alqosh in northern Iraq that bears his name. The monastery was for centuries the residence of East Syriac patriarchs and remains a great centre of pilgrimage for Assyrians and Chaldeans.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabban_Hormizd_Monastery']},
    's_rafqa': {'en': 'St Rafqa', 'text': "Rafqa (Rebecca) Pietra Choboq Ar-Rayès (1832-1914) was a Lebanese Maronite nun who, after years of teaching, entered the Lebanese Maronite Order. Tradition holds that she asked to share in Christ's sufferings and then lived for nearly thirty years with blindness, paralysis and pain, accepted with patience. She was canonised in 2001.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rafqa_Pietra_Choboq_Ar-Ray%C3%A8s']},
    's_raphael_archangel': {'en': 'St Raphael the Archangel', 'text': "Raphael is the archangel of the Book of Tobit who guides the young Tobias and heals his father's blindness; his name means 'God heals'. He is honoured as a healer and protector of travellers. In the Ethiopian tradition the 13th of every month is dedicated to him (Rufa'el).", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raphael_(archangel)', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/13_3', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Pagumen#Pagumen3']},
    's_raphael_of_brooklyn': {'en': 'St Raphael of Brooklyn', 'text': 'Syrian-born priest Raphael Hawaweeny (1860-1915) who came to New York to serve Arab Orthodox immigrants and in 1904 became the first Orthodox bishop consecrated in North America. He founded St Nicholas Cathedral in Brooklyn and dozens of parishes; he was glorified in 2000.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raphael_of_Brooklyn']},
    's_romanos_melodist': {'en': 'St Romanos the Melodist', 'text': 'Hymnographer from Emesa (Homs) in Syria who served as a deacon in Constantinople in the 6th century. Tradition holds that the Theotokos gave him the gift of song in a dream, after which he composed the Christmas kontakion and many other long verse sermons; he is the patron of church singers.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanos_the_Melodist']},
    's_sabbas': {'en': 'St Sabbas the Sanctified', 'text': 'Cappadocian monk (439-532) who founded the Great Lavra of Mar Saba in the Judaean desert, still inhabited today. A disciple of Euthymius, he led the hermits of Palestine and defended the Council of Chalcedon; the Jerusalem typikon of his lavra became the basis of Byzantine daily worship.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sabbas_the_Sanctified']},
    's_sahak_and_mesrop': {'en': 'Sts Sahak and Mesrop', 'text': "Catholicos Sahak Partev (c. 348-439) and the monk Mesrop Mashtots (c. 362-440) together created the Armenian alphabet around AD 405 and led the translation of the Bible into Armenian. Their work founded Armenian literature and made worship in the people's own language possible. The Armenian Church honours them as the first of the Holy Translators.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mesrop_Mashtots', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isaac_of_Armenia']},
    's_saints_of_north_america': {'en': 'Synaxis of the Saints of North America', 'text': 'Commemoration of all saints who shone in North America, among them Herman and Innocent of Alaska, Tikhon of Moscow, Alexis Toth, Raphael of Brooklyn and the martyrs Peter the Aleut and Juvenal. The Orthodox Church in America keeps it on the second Sunday after Pentecost.', 'sources': ['https://www.oca.org/saints/lives/2026/06/14']},
    's_samuel_of_waldebba': {'en': 'St Samuel of Waldebba', 'text': 'Samuel of Waldebba (late 14th-early 15th century) was a monk and hermit who founded the monastic centre of Waldebba in the lowlands of north-west Ethiopia. Tradition shows him riding a lion. Waldebba is still renowned for the strictness of its ascetic life.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_of_Waldebba', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Tahisas#Tahisas12']},
    's_samuel_the_confessor': {'en': 'St Samuel the Confessor', 'text': "Samuel of Kalamon (c. 597-695) was a monk of Scetis who resisted the Chalcedonian definition imposed by the patriarch Cyrus; tradition holds that he lost an eye from the beating he received. He later founded the monastery at Qalamun in the Fayoum, which bears his name. The Coptic Church calls him 'the Confessor'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_the_Confessor', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/4_8', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Tahisas#Tahisas8']},
    's_sarkis_the_warrior': {'en': 'St Sarkis the Warrior', 'text': "Sarkis (Sergius) was, according to Armenian tradition, a Christian general of the 4th century who served under Constantine and was martyred in Persia with his son Martiros and fourteen soldiers. He is one of the best-loved saints of the Armenian Church and is regarded as a patron of young people and of love. A popular custom on the eve of his feast is to eat salted bread so that one's future spouse may appear in a dream.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Sarkis_the_Warrior', 'https://armenianchurch.us/way-of-a-warrior-st-sarkis-6/']},
    's_sava_of_serbia': {'en': 'St Sava of Serbia', 'text': 'Prince Rastko Nemanjić (c. 1174-1236), who became a monk on Mount Athos, founded the Hilandar monastery with his father and in 1219 became the first Archbishop of an autocephalous Serbian Church. Honoured as Enlightener of the Serbs, he is patron of Serbian schools, and his feast, Savindan, is a school holiday in Serbia.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Sava']},
    's_scholastica': {'en': 'St Scholastica', 'text': 'Scholastica (c. 480–543) was, by tradition, the twin sister of St Benedict, and led a community of consecrated women near his monastery of Monte Cassino. St Gregory the Great tells that at their last meeting her prayer brought a storm that kept Benedict talking with her through the night. She is honoured as the patron of Benedictine nuns.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scholastica']},
    's_seraphim_of_sarov': {'en': 'St Seraphim of Sarov', 'text': "Russian monk (1754-1833) of the Sarov monastery who after years of solitude and prayer became a much-sought elder, greeting every visitor with 'Christ is risen, my joy!'. He taught that the aim of the Christian life is the acquisition of the Holy Spirit; he was glorified in 1903 and is one of the most loved saints of modern Orthodoxy.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seraphim_of_Sarov']},
    's_sergius_and_bacchus': {'en': 'Sts Sergius and Bacchus', 'text': 'Sergius and Bacchus were Roman officers martyred in Syria in the early 4th century; tradition holds that Sergius was beheaded at Resafa, which became a major pilgrimage city named Sergiopolis. They were among the most popular saints of the Syriac-speaking Christians and of the Arab Christian tribes. Many churches in the Middle East bear their names.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergius_and_Bacchus']},
    's_sergius_and_herman_valaam': {'en': 'Sts Sergius and Herman of Valaam', 'text': 'Founders of the Valaam monastery on Lake Ladoga, whose lives are known only from late tradition; historical sources suggest that the monastery was founded around 1400. Honoured as Enlighteners of Karelia, they are among the most venerated saints of the Finnish Orthodox Church, and New Valaam in Finland continues their monastery.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergius_and_Herman_of_Valaam', 'https://ort.fi/kirkkokalenteri/20250628/']},
    's_sergius_of_radonezh': {'en': 'St Sergius of Radonezh', 'text': "Russian monk (c. 1314-1392) who founded the Holy Trinity Lavra north of Moscow and renewed communal monastic life in Russia; his disciples founded dozens of monasteries. Tradition holds that he blessed Prince Dmitry Donskoy before the battle of Kulikovo in 1380, and he is honoured as the 'Abbot of all Russia' and a patron of the nation.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergius_of_Radonezh']},
    's_seven_apostles_bulgaria': {'en': 'The Seven Saints (Sedmochislenitsi)', 'text': 'Joint commemoration of Sts Cyril and Methodius and their five disciples Clement, Naum, Gorazd, Angelarius and Sava, who carried Slavonic letters and liturgy to Bulgaria after their expulsion from Moravia. They are honoured as Equals-to-the-Apostles and Enlighteners of the Slavs.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seven_Apostles_of_Bulgaria']},
    's_severus_of_antioch': {'en': 'St Severus of Antioch', 'text': 'Severus (c. 465-538) was patriarch of Antioch from 512 and the leading theologian of the opposition to the Council of Chalcedon. Deposed in 518, he spent his last years in Egypt, where he is buried. The Oriental Orthodox churches venerate him as a father of their Christology and of the Syriac tradition.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Severus_of_Antioch', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/6_14', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Yekatit#Yekatit14']},
    's_sharbel': {'en': 'St Sharbel Makhluf', 'text': "Sharbel (Charbel) Makhluf (1828–1898) was a Maronite monk and priest of Lebanon who spent his last twenty-three years as a hermit at the hermitage of Sts Peter and Paul above the monastery of Annaya. Reports of healings at his tomb drew pilgrims of many faiths. Canonised in 1977, he is Lebanon's best-known saint and his optional memorial is in the General Roman Calendar.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charbel_Makhlouf']},
    's_shenoute': {'en': 'St Shenoute the Archimandrite', 'text': 'Shenoute (d. c. 465) was abbot of the White Monastery near Sohag in Upper Egypt, which grew into a federation of thousands of monks and nuns. His sermons and letters are the classic works of Sahidic Coptic literature. Copts call him Anba Shenouda, the Archimandrite.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shenoute', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/11_7', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Hamle#Hamle7']},
    's_shimun_bar_sabbae': {'en': 'St Shimun bar Sabbae', 'text': 'Shimun bar Sabbae was Catholicos of the Church of the East at Seleucia-Ctesiphon who was executed in 341 (some sources give 344) with many clergy at the beginning of the great persecution of Christians under the Persian king Shapur II. The Church of the East honours him as the leader of its martyrs. He is also commemorated by the Greek and Latin churches.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shemon_bar_Sabbae']},
    's_shmoni_and_her_sons': {'en': 'St Shmoni and her Seven Sons', 'text': 'Shmoni (Solomonia) is the Syriac name of the mother of the seven brothers who, according to 2 Maccabees, were tortured to death with their teacher Eleazar under King Antiochus IV around 167 BC for refusing to break the law of God. The Syriac churches honour them as martyrs of the Old Covenant whose courage foreshadows the Christian martyrs.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holy_Maccabean_Martyrs']},
    's_shushanik': {'en': 'St Shushanik', 'text': 'Shushanik (Shushan), daughter of St Vardan Mamikonian, was married to Varsken, a prince of Gugark who renounced Christianity for Zoroastrianism. Refusing to follow him, she endured years of imprisonment and abuse and died around AD 475. Her passion, written by her confessor, is the oldest surviving work of Georgian literature, and she is honoured by both the Armenian and Georgian Churches.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shushanik', 'https://armenianchurch.us/the-saints/st-shooshan-the-martyr/']},
    's_simeon_nemanja': {'en': 'St Simeon the Myrrh-streaming (Stefan Nemanja)', 'text': 'Grand Prince of Serbia (c. 1113-1199) and founder of the Nemanjić dynasty, who abdicated in 1196, became the monk Simeon and with his son Sava founded the Hilandar monastery on Mount Athos. Tradition holds that fragrant myrrh flowed from his relics, which rest in the Studenica monastery.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stefan_Nemanja']},
    's_simeon_stylites': {'en': 'St Simeon the Stylite', 'text': "Simeon (c. 390-459) was a Syrian ascetic who spent about 37 years living on top of a pillar near Aleppo, praying and counselling the crowds who came to him. He was the first and best-known of the stylites, and his example was followed by many later pillar-dwellers. The ruins of the great church built around his pillar, Qal'at Sim'an, survive in northern Syria.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simeon_Stylites']},
    's_simon_and_jude': {'en': 'Sts Simon and Jude', 'text': "Simon, called the Zealot, and Jude (Thaddaeus), called in Luke 'Judas son of James', were two of the Twelve Apostles about whom the New Testament says little. Western tradition holds that they preached together in Persia and were martyred there, which is why they share one day in the Latin calendar. Jude is traditionally linked with the Letter of Jude and is popularly invoked as patron of desperate causes.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jude_the_Apostle']},
    's_simon_zealot': {'en': 'St Simon the Zealot', 'text': "One of the Twelve, called 'the Zealot' or 'the Canaanite' to distinguish him from Simon Peter. Byzantine tradition identifies him with the bridegroom of the wedding at Cana and holds that he preached around the Black Sea and was martyred in Abkhazia, where a cave and church at New Athos bear his name.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simon_the_Zealot']},
    's_spyridon': {'en': 'St Spyridon of Trimythous', 'text': 'Shepherd who became Bishop of Trimythous in Cyprus (c. 270-348) and, tradition holds, confounded a philosopher at the Council of Nicaea by squeezing a brick into fire, water and clay as an image of the Trinity. His incorrupt relics rest in Corfu, whose patron he is, and he is invoked as a wonderworker across the Greek world.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spyridon']},
    's_stanislaus': {'en': 'St Stanislaus', 'text': 'Stanislaus of Szczepanów (c. 1030–1079) was bishop of Kraków. Tradition holds that he rebuked King Bolesław II for injustice and immorality and was killed by the king, or at his order, while celebrating Mass. He is a principal patron of Poland, and his relics rest in Wawel Cathedral.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanislaus_of_Szczepanów']},
    's_stephen': {'en': 'St Stephen', 'text': 'Stephen was one of the seven deacons chosen by the apostles in Jerusalem to serve the community, as told in the Acts of the Apostles. Accused of blasphemy, he was stoned to death after a speech before the Sanhedrin, praying for his killers, while the young Saul (later St Paul) looked on. He is honoured as the Protomartyr, the first Christian martyr, and as an archdeacon.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Stephen', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/5_1']},
    's_takla_haymanot': {'en': 'St Takla Haymanot', 'text': 'Takla Haymanot (c. 1215-1313) was a monk and preacher from Shewa who evangelised central Ethiopia and founded the monastery of Debre Libanos, whose abbot, the Echege, became the leading monastic office of the Ethiopian Church. Icons show him with wings and a single leg, recalling the tradition that he stood in prayer until one leg withered. The 24th of every Ethiopian month is dedicated to him, and he is honoured also by the Coptic church and by the Catholic churches of these rites.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekle_Haymanot', 'https://eotcmk.org/e/the-feast-of-abune-tekle-haimanots-departure/', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/12_24']},
    's_tamar_of_georgia': {'en': 'St Tamar, Queen of Georgia', 'text': "Queen of Georgia (r. 1184-1213) whose reign marked the height of the medieval Georgian kingdom in power and culture. A patron of churches and monasteries, she is honoured by the Georgian Church as the 'Holy Righteous King Tamar'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamar_of_Georgia']},
    's_teresa_benedicta_of_the_cross': {'en': 'St Teresa Benedicta of the Cross (Edith Stein)', 'text': 'Edith Stein (1891–1942), born to a Jewish family in Breslau, was a philosopher and student of Edmund Husserl who became a Catholic in 1922 and later a Discalced Carmelite nun as Teresa Benedicta of the Cross. Arrested in the Netherlands by the Nazis, she was murdered at Auschwitz with her sister Rosa. Canonised in 1998, she was named a co-patron of Europe in 1999.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edith_Stein']},
    's_teresa_of_avila': {'en': 'St Teresa of Ávila', 'text': 'Teresa of Jesus (1515–1582), a Carmelite nun of Ávila in Spain, reformed her order by founding convents of Discalced Carmelites and, with St John of the Cross, extended the reform to friars. Her writings, including her Life, The Way of Perfection and The Interior Castle, are classics of mystical prayer. In 1970 she became the first woman declared a Doctor of the Church.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teresa_of_Ávila']},
    's_teresa_of_calcutta': {'en': 'St Teresa of Calcutta', 'text': "Anjezë Gonxhe Bojaxhiu (1910–1997), born in Skopje, was a Loreto Sister teaching in Calcutta when she felt called to serve 'the poorest of the poor'. In 1950 she founded the Missionaries of Charity, who care for the dying, abandoned children and people with leprosy around the world, and she received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1979. Canonised in 2016, she was added to the General Roman Calendar in 2025 on the anniversary of her death.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mother_Teresa']},
    's_thaddaeus_apostle': {'en': 'St Thaddaeus the Apostle', 'text': 'Thaddaeus is named among the Twelve in Matthew and Mark; many traditions identify him with Jude, son of James. Eastern traditions credit him with preaching in Syria and Mesopotamia. In the Ethiopian tradition the 2nd of every month is dedicated to him.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jude_the_Apostle', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/11_2']},
    's_thaddeus_and_bartholomew': {'en': 'Sts Thaddeus and Bartholomew', 'text': "The apostles Thaddeus and Bartholomew are honoured by the Armenian Church as the first preachers of Christianity in Armenia in the 1st century, and tradition holds that both were martyred there. Their mission is the basis of the Church's claim to apostolic origin, and the Armenian Church is sometimes called after them. Their traditional tombs are associated with the monastery of St Thaddeus in north-west Iran and with Bartholomew's shrine at Albac (Başkale).", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thaddeus_of_Edessa', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bartholomew_the_Apostle', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armenian_Apostolic_Church']},
    's_thecla': {'en': 'St Thecla', 'text': 'Disciple of the Apostle Paul from Iconium whose story is told in the second-century Acts of Paul and Thecla: tradition holds that she survived fire and wild beasts and lived as a hermit near Seleucia. The East calls her Protomartyr among women and Equal-to-the-Apostles, and her shrine at Maaloula in Syria remains a place of pilgrimage.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thecla']},
    's_theodore_romzha': {'en': 'Bl. Theodore Romzha', 'text': 'Greek Catholic Bishop of Mukachevo (1911-1947) who led his church in Transcarpathia as the Soviets tried to force it into the Russian Orthodox Church. After a staged road accident he was poisoned in hospital; he was beatified as a martyr by Pope John Paul II in 2001.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodore_Romzha']},
    's_theodore_tyro': {'en': 'St Theodore the Recruit (Tyron)', 'text': 'Soldier of Amasea in Pontus martyred by fire around 306. Byzantine tradition holds that in 362 he appeared to the Archbishop of Constantinople and warned the faithful not to buy market food that Julian the Apostate had sprinkled with sacrificial blood, telling them to eat boiled wheat instead; the first Saturday of Great Lent recalls this with blessed kollyva.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodore_of_Amasea']},
    's_theodosius_cenobiarch': {'en': 'St Theodosius the Cenobiarch', 'text': 'Palestinian monk (c. 423-529) who founded a great community (koinobion) in the Judaean desert near Bethlehem, with guesthouses and hospitals for pilgrims, the sick and the elderly. He was named head of all the cenobitic monks of Palestine, beside St Sabbas for the hermits, and supported the Council of Chalcedon.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodosius_the_Cenobiarch']},
    's_theodosius_of_kyiv': {'en': 'St Theodosius of the Kyiv Caves', 'text': "Abbot of the Kyiv Caves monastery (d. 1074) who introduced the Studite rule of common life, built the first stone church and cared for the poor of Kyiv. With St Anthony he is honoured as a founder of monastic life in Rus'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodosius_of_Kiev']},
    's_therese_of_lisieux': {'en': 'St Thérèse of the Child Jesus', 'text': "Thérèse Martin (1873–1897) entered the Carmel of Lisieux in Normandy at fifteen and died of tuberculosis at twenty-four. Her autobiography, Story of a Soul, describing her 'little way' of trust and love in ordinary things, became one of the most widely read spiritual books of the twentieth century. She is a Doctor of the Church, patron of missions and co-patron of France; she is often called the 'Little Flower'.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thérèse_of_Lisieux']},
    's_thomas_apostle': {'en': 'St Thomas the Apostle', 'text': "Thomas, called Didymus ('the Twin'), was one of the Twelve Apostles. In John's Gospel he doubts the resurrection until he sees the risen Christ, then confesses 'My Lord and my God'. Tradition holds that he preached in Mesopotamia, Persia and India and was martyred near Mylapore (Chennai); the Saint Thomas Christians of India trace their Church to him, and Edessa long kept his relics.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_the_Apostle', 'https://copticchurch.net/synaxarium/9_26', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Thomas_Christians']},
    's_thomas_aquinas': {'en': 'St Thomas Aquinas', 'text': 'Thomas Aquinas (c. 1225–1274) was an Italian Dominican friar who taught at Paris and Naples. His Summa Theologiae joined Aristotelian philosophy with Christian theology and became the standard framework of Catholic theological teaching. He also wrote the hymns for the feast of Corpus Christi. A Doctor of the Church, he is called the Angelic Doctor and is patron of Catholic universities and schools.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Aquinas']},
    's_thomas_becket': {'en': 'St Thomas Becket', 'text': "Thomas Becket (c. 1119–1170) was chancellor and friend of King Henry II of England before becoming archbishop of Canterbury in 1162. He then defended the rights of the Church against the king, went into exile, and on his return was murdered by four knights in Canterbury Cathedral. Canonised in 1173, he became one of the most venerated saints of medieval Europe, and his shrine was the goal of the pilgrims in Chaucer's Canterbury Tales.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Becket']},
    's_three_hierarchs': {'en': 'Synaxis of the Three Hierarchs', 'text': 'A joint feast of Basil the Great, Gregory the Theologian and John Chrysostom, the three great teachers of the Greek Church. Tradition holds that it was set in the 11th century to settle a dispute among the faithful about which of the three was greatest. In Greece the day is also honoured as a feast of education and letters.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Three_Holy_Hierarchs']},
    's_tikhon_of_moscow': {'en': 'St Tikhon of Moscow', 'text': 'Vasily Bellavin (1865-1925), archbishop of the North American diocese from 1898 to 1907 and in 1917 the first Patriarch of Moscow elected after the restoration of the patriarchate. He led the Russian Church through the early Soviet persecution, was imprisoned and died under pressure; he was glorified in 1989 and is also honoured as Enlightener of North America.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patriarch_Tikhon_of_Moscow']},
    's_timothy_and_titus': {'en': 'Sts Timothy and Titus', 'text': "Timothy and Titus were companions and fellow workers of St Paul in the first century. Tradition holds that Timothy became the first bishop of Ephesus and Titus the first bishop of Crete. Three letters of the New Testament, the Pastoral Epistles, are addressed to them, which is why they are remembered together the day after Paul's conversion.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Timothy']},
    's_tiridates_ashkhen_khosrovidukht': {'en': 'Sts Tiridates, Ashkhen and Khosrovidukht', 'text': "King Tiridates III of Armenia, his queen Ashkhen and his sister Khosrovidukht are honoured as the royal saints of Armenia's conversion. Tradition holds that the king, after persecuting Gregory the Illuminator and the virgin martyrs, was healed and baptised by Gregory, making Armenia a Christian kingdom in the early 4th century. Khosrovidukht is remembered for the dream that led to Gregory's release from the pit.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiridates_III_of_Armenia', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashkhen']},
    's_tryphon_of_pechenga': {'en': 'St Tryphon of Pechenga', 'text': "Novgorodian missionary (c. 1495-1583) who preached to the Sami people of the Kola Peninsula and founded the Pechenga monastery in the far north. He is honoured as Enlightener of the Lapps (Sami) and is a patron of the Finnish Orthodox Church's northern parishes.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tryphon_of_Pechenga']},
    's_twelve_apostles': {'en': 'Synaxis of the Twelve Apostles', 'text': 'A joint commemoration of the Twelve on the day after the feast of Peter and Paul, honouring the whole apostolic college whose preaching founded the churches. Byzantine tradition counts Peter, Andrew, James and John (sons of Zebedee), Philip, Bartholomew, Thomas, Matthew, James son of Alphaeus, Jude, Simon the Zealot and Matthias.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apostles_in_the_New_Testament']},
    's_uriel_archangel': {'en': 'St Uriel the Archangel', 'text': "Uriel (Ura'el) is an archangel known from the Book of Enoch and other texts that belong to the Ethiopian biblical canon, where he guides Enoch through the heavens. The Ethiopian Church honours him as one of the archangels who stand before God. The 22nd of every Ethiopian month carries his commemoration.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uriel', 'https://www.eotc-ma.com/beliefs-and-origins-order-of-calend']},
    's_vardanants': {'en': 'St Vardan and his Companions (Vardanants)', 'text': 'Vardanants commemorates Vardan Mamikonian and the 1,036 soldiers who fell at the Battle of Avarayr in AD 451, resisting the Persian attempt to impose Zoroastrianism on Armenia. Although the battle was lost, the Church regards their deaths as martyrdom that secured the freedom to remain Christian. The Thursday before Great Lent is both a church feast and a national day of remembrance.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vardanants_War', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Avarayr']},
    's_vasil_hopko': {'en': 'Bl. Vasil Hopko', 'text': 'Auxiliary bishop of the Greek Catholic Eparchy of Prešov (1904-1976), imprisoned and tortured by the communist authorities after 1950, who died from the effects of his imprisonment. He was beatified as a martyr in 2003.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basil_Hopko']},
    's_vattasseril_dionysius': {'en': 'St Dionysius of Vattasseril', 'text': "Geevarghese Mar Dionysius of Vattasseril (1858-1934), Malankara Metropolitan from 1909, led the Malankara Church through the struggles that resulted in the establishment of the Catholicate of the East in India in 1912. Known as 'Malankara Sabha Bhasuran', the great luminary of the Malankara Church, he is buried at the Old Seminary in Kottayam. The Malankara Orthodox Syrian Church declared him a saint in 2003.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dionysius_Vattasseril', 'https://mosc.in/saints/st-geevarghese-mar-dionysius-vattasseril/']},
    's_vincent_de_paul': {'en': 'St Vincent de Paul', 'text': 'Vincent de Paul (1581–1660), a French priest of peasant origins, devoted his life to the poor, the sick, galley slaves and abandoned children. He founded the Congregation of the Mission (Vincentians) and, with St Louise de Marillac, the Daughters of Charity, who worked outside the cloister among the poor. He is patron of charitable societies, and the Society of St Vincent de Paul, founded in 1833, bears his name.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vincent_de_Paul']},
    's_vladimir_great': {'en': 'St Vladimir the Great', 'text': "Grand Prince of Kyiv (c. 958-1015) who was baptised around 988 and brought the Christian faith of Byzantium to his people, with the mass baptism of Kyivans in the Dnieper. Honoured as Equal-to-the-Apostles and baptiser of Rus', he is claimed as a founder by the churches of Ukraine, Russia and Belarus, and his feast is a state holiday in Ukraine.", 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vladimir_the_Great']},
    's_walatta_petros': {'en': 'St Walatta Petros', 'text': 'Walatta Petros (1592-1642) was a noblewoman who left her husband to lead resistance to the Catholicism imposed by King Susenyos, and founded religious communities of men and women around Lake Tana. Her seventeenth-century Life is one of the earliest biographies of an African woman. She is venerated as a saint by the Ethiopian Orthodox Church.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walatta_Petros', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Hedar#Hedar17']},
    's_xenia_of_petersburg': {'en': 'St Xenia of St Petersburg', 'text': 'Widow (c. 1719-c. 1803) who after the sudden death of her husband gave away her property and lived as a fool-for-Christ on the streets of St Petersburg, wearing his clothes and answering to his name. Tradition credits her with prayers and foresight that helped the poor, and her chapel at the Smolensk cemetery is a place of pilgrimage.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xenia_of_Saint_Petersburg']},
    's_yared': {'en': 'St Yared', 'text': 'Yared (6th century) was a priest and musician of Aksum who, according to tradition, received the sacred chant of the Ethiopian Church through a heavenly vision. He is credited with composing the Deggwa hymnary and with the three modes of Ethiopian liturgical chant. He is honoured as the father of Ethiopian church music.', 'sources': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yared', 'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian_church/Ginbot#Ginbot11', 'https://eotcmk.org/e/the-feast-of-the-departure-of-st-yared/']},
}

